This is going a weird place.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.920
Contra is a run-and-gun game.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.660
Which we have not had on Nostalgia before, but it basically feels like a shmup, right?

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.060
What is your guys' familiarity with run-and-gun?

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.080
It sort of felt like Commando.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.400
Okay. Like Commando was a very bad version of what this would turn into.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.820
Or like Ikari Warriors almost, but the other way.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.380
Yeah. Ikari Warriors from the side.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.280
Yeah, well, I feel like those two had...

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.620
I don't know. They felt slower in pace in nature, whereas this feels more like you're constantly shooting,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:39.920
you're constantly moving.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.860
Action packed. There's a lot of things on the screen coming at you.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.060
I remember Ikari Warriors couldn't even handle that many things on the screen at once.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.220
Yeah. There's something about it being so packed with stuff, and also it being controllable enough

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:59.260
that it makes it like a tense run-and-gun experience, where I think the other ones fell short a little bit.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:06.220
Yeah, and Wikipedia describes a run-and-gun as a shoot-em-up in which the protagonists fight on foot,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.300
perhaps with the ability to jump.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:13.400
Run-and-gun games may use side-scrolling, vertical-scrolling, or isometric viewpoints,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.580
and may feature multidirectional movement.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.840
Per chance to be able to jump.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.780
Yes. And run-and-gun games that I can think of are things like Metal Slug.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.400
Cuphead is a run-and-gun game.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:27.360
Earth Defense Force?

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.920
Does that count? I think that counts.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.300
A cuphead, you're not on...

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.880
Oh, you're on foot, yeah, for a lot of levels.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.320
You're a human man. I'm just thinking about the flying levels.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.580
Sorry if we said this. Did we say Mega Man?

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.480
Do you would consider Mega Man a run-and-gun?

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.800
I think it's a little bit slower. I think it's a platformer.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.600
Yeah, it's a platformer, but slower, but also, I mean, you run, you gun.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.120
Yeah, I mean, I guess that's tough, because I wouldn't, in my head,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.680
I wouldn't think Mega Man platformer at all.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.820
Yeah, based on that description, technically...

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.960
Is Super Mario a brother?

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.500
I would say...

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.840
The Fire Flower? The lines between genres are sort of fluid.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.660
Yeah, exactly. We need to clearly define it right now, Sean.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.180
All right, let's do it. No, it's fine.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.260
Oh. We should talk about Contra, which the objective, basically, of Contra

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.480
is just to platform your way through seven levels

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.160
and shoot down aliens and other enemies, I say,

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.360
because it's unclear if they're just, like, people of Earth

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.420
or alien henchmen. That have, like, been recruited by the aliens.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.480
Who would take the alien side?

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.440
Who they are, but you have to pretty much just basically shoot down everything in your way.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.620
There's no way really around things.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.200
Should there have been a diplomatic option?

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.220
There is no diplomatic option, but...

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.000
But are you saying, should there have been?

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.660
I think there should have been maybe a pacifist option, too.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.220
You pretty much have to kill everything.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.940
You can't. A stealth option. There's very few things you can just, like, jump over and continue about on your way.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.640
True. So let's get into first impressions.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.960
Joe, why don't you take us through your first impression playing Contra?

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.860
This is one of the rare games that I didn't have a blind spot to.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.480
I actually have played this as a kid.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.780
Was not very good at it, particularly when I was younger,

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.760
but I went in knowing a little bit about it, remembering it a little bit.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.700
I already had pretty high expectations for it, because I remember liking it,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.980
and I know it has a lot of positive reception.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.480
It actually, off the bat, exceeded my expectations.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.480
It was more fun than I remembered, and, you know,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.920
I just immediately kind of got absorbed in it.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.200
All right, cool. Sean?

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:27.980
I also knew about Contra, and I had played maybe the 16-bit versions of this game,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.960
but right off the bat, what I noticed was this game looks really good,

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:39.140
whether it's the background art or the actual sprites for your character.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.440
Like, this is the first time that it actually kind of feels like an 80s action movie.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.200
So I was also pleasantly surprised with that.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.400
All right, Sam? I had played this game before somehow.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:56.120
I don't remember exactly which version of it it was, you know, just a couple minutes of it.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.160
And kind of the same as Joe.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.300
I was surprised at how, you know, just how solid it was.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.320
Like, the movement was good.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.260
But one thing that we haven't mentioned is it sounds cool, too.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.540
Like, the one thing I noticed immediately was that the gun sounds really cool to fire.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.580
That's true. Yeah.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.920
Yeah, and I think another reason to the benefit of, like, playing games this way,

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.480
the way we do it on Nostalgia, where we play them chronologically,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.880
is that you really see the surprise that a game like this can bring.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.300
Whereas if you're just kind of, like, shuffling through the NES as an afterthought,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:32.340
and you play some other games later, this one might not, like, surprise you as much.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.500
But this is the first time.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.080
I mean, this is 93.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.220
And this is a very impressive game.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.240
93? No, game 93.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.440
Oh, game 93.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.160
Yeah, like, 93 games in, and I'm still being surprised by, like, ways that the games can play.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.820
And to Sean's point, like, I was blown away by the fluidness of the animation

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.940
and the fact that you were able to do the diagonal attack,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.980
something that we talked about in Castlevania,

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.440
that we wanted Simon to be able to, like, strike the whip in multiple directions,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.800
something he wouldn't get until Super Castlevania on the Super Nintendo.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.760
So to have all those things, like, in this game from the very start,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.700
it was a flawless presentation at the start of it.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:14.720
And it even starts cool.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.200
You, like, you pit start on the game, you get, like, a scorecard or something,

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.360
it says, like, player one start,

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:26.740
and then you do, like, a triple front flip and just land in the jungle.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.520
And it's like, wow, what a way to enter the game

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.040
and know that you're about to kick some butt.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.940
Yeah, and to mention something about how clean it all looks,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.400
this is something that I never thought of before,

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.300
but it's always been, like, subconsciously there,

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:43.000
that some games that I don't like that I think feel weird to play

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.900
are games where the platforms are not super clear where you can land

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.420
and what's a platform. And this is, like, everything is crisp and clear,

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.460
and you know what you're standing on, where you can go, where you can jump.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.340
Nothing's confusing in this game.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.580
We've had that problem very recently with both Karnov and Wizards and Warriors,

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.500
where it was kind of like some things,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.460
it just doesn't seem like they should be platforms,

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:03.380
and you're standing on them,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.040
and other things, you want them to be platforms, and they're not.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.660
I will say, you know, we're getting a bit into gameplay here,

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:13.040
but there are some problems with conscious platforming as well, though,

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:17.300
where you have to get, like, right to the edge of the current platform

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.720
to make a successful jump, and I never like that.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.200
I'm not really a fan of that kind of thing.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.340
One other first impression that I have was that I noticed that there's, like,

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:29.560
a demo mode if you wait on the home screen for too long.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.340
Oh, yeah, and we haven't had that.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:32.300
I don't think we've ever had that.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.500
Yeah, I think we have.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.440
Yeah, well, I'm trying to think of what it was.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.880
I don't remember either. I think it was Karate Champ, not at Karate Champ.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:40.740
Yeah, what's the one we love, the backgrounds?

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.140
Oh, Karate Champ. I'm pretty sure Karate Champ had a demo play.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.400
Yeah, and I really like that too, Sam.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:47.920
That's a good call. Yeah, that was just something.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.360
Because you never, you know, you never get, like, that in the older games,

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.340
but I remember even in, like, N64 games, which is what I grew up on,

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.500
sometimes you would watch those just to get ideas of what to do.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.580
I remember Donkey Kong 64 had those.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.500
Yeah, I never really thought to do that.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.860
I always just got annoyed that it started making noise

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.960
when I was just waiting on, like, the main menu.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.360
And it's like, wait, I want to make my selection.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.800
Well, this game does that a lot.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.520
You try and put in the little Konami code there,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.740
you get, like, a few seconds before that cutscene comes up.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:17.760
Yeah, yeah. Right.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.540
All right, so before we go any further in gameplay,

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.880
let's talk about some Tales from the Manual.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:27.060
One thing to note is that the Contra was first on the Arcade,

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.620
and so the plot's a little different on the NES version.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:35.780
For those of you in the know, the Arcade plot is that in 2633 AD,

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:41.900
two armed elite Marine commandos, Contra, named Bill and Lance,

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.500
are sent on a mission to neutralize a terrorist organization called Red Falcon

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.240
that is secretly planning to take over the entire world,

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:55.080
and it is up to this two-man army to put an end to this madness once and for all.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:00.100
You know, the fact that aliens don't come up in this plot description is a little strange,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.040
but it does help understand why there are, like, also, like, human figures

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.380
that you fight in the game, too, because Red Falcon is, like, the terrorist organization,

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.300
and then it just happens to be that they're in cahoots with aliens

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.080
who are helping take over the world.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.480
But the plot is a little different in the NES game,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.440
and so, Joe, why don't you walk us through what the differences are

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.520
for the NES version of Contra?

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.760
Sure. So, like you said, it's a little more alien-based, which was surprising.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.440
But in the NES version, going back to 1957,

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.860
an extraterrestrial object crash-landed into Earth,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.040
and Earth scientists just passed it off as a nothing event,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.280
and they forgot about it.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.840
Thirty years later, now in 1987, 1988, whenever,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.620
there are reports of an evil force appearing in the Amazon,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.500
which I think is funny that they consider them inherently evil,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.840
the United States government, I guess?

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:58.040
Yeah. The Pentagon.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.260
Oh, the Pentagon.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.620
Okay. The Pentagon sends special forces,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.140
and congratulations, you're those special forces,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.000
is what the manual says in the middle of the story.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.660
It suddenly shifts to, congratulations, pal,

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:12.180
you're one of the chosen ones.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:16.160
You're sent in to take out the alien invaders.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.180
One other thing the manual mentions,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.860
that if you succeed, well, it doesn't matter,

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.500
because I doubt you will.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:27.460
I love that, like, that fight from the manual, you know,

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.640
like, you won't pull it off, coward.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.260
Yeah, exactly. And those are just, like, the cliff notes of it.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.000
It actually is a pretty in-depth story,

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.140
which I recommend you read,

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:37.580
because there's a lot of detail to it.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.000
I really like the part where they justify

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.220
why there's only a two-man team going into the Amazon game force.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.780
Yeah, yeah. This is the first time that I've actually sort of, like,

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:48.300
bought the manual story.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:52.940
It's not about some bullshit in 32XX with a planet Gore Blacks

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:54.040
that's going to eat everybody.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.080
If it's more powerful, the more people attack it or something.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.280
This is some, like, Cold War stuff with aliens,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.520
and I think it's awesome that they actually explain, like,

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:06.440
we just don't want the economies to get all messed up,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.800
so it's just going to be you two.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.660
Right. We don't want to start a war.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.640
But you just know that there's aliens there.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.300
Do you think that there'd be, like,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.880
some kind of talks going on with the Russians?

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.360
Be like, yo.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.660
Or the Brazilians. Be like, yo, guys, there's aliens.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.320
Like, I get that we don't like each other, but, like, come on.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.200
Like, it's skeet it, right? They're trying to kill us all.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.100
And we should probably talk now, too,

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.400
about, like, the influences of, like, movies at that time

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.020
that would have made Contra popular

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.860
because this definitely has, like, a Rambo meets Alien kind of,

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.520
or Aliens kind of feel to it

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.920
because Alien is, I always think of that as more of, like,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.420
a horror film and Aliens is more like a Colonel Marines.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.400
The design, though, is still either or.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.360
Okay, yeah. It's super, like, knockoff H.R.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:56.360
Geiger when you get to the more alien levels.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.720
Yes. Later in the game.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:11:58.760
And what is that H.R.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.600
Geiger? That is the designer that designed the alien itself.

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.160
Okay, because that's what I'm always thinking of is, like,

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.640
do those aliens have names?

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:08.240
Yeah, it's a xenomorph.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:12.020
Xenomorph. He also designed all of the weird, like,

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:16.740
sort of, like, temple-looking structures throughout the movies.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.040
Got it. Because the xenomorph thing, at first I was like,

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:21.060
oh, okay, it's aliens,

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:24.800
and I get, like, the illusions that they make and stuff like that.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:26.380
But by the end of it, you're right.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:29.540
I think I saw a xenomorph at this game at one point or another

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:30.500
towards the end of it.

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.680
But you also get the Rambo feel with the heroes who have changed.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.600
They're now codenamed Scorpion and Mad Dog.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.600
Mad Dog. Mad Dog Bill.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:43.880
And that gives me more of, like, a Rambo feel with the way they look

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.720
and they're, you know, they have some big 80s muscles.

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:51.060
Especially the Scorpion. Yeah, Scorpion looks a little bit like Al Pacino meets Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.640
I think he's got a Sylvester Stallone look to him.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:55.020
Oh, Sylvester Stallone. That's who I meant.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:00.480
So then I assumed that Bill was supposed to be Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.800
Okay. But he doesn't look as much like it as Lance looks like.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.300
He looks like a Joe Pesci to me.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:08.060
He looks kind of... No, who's that guy from Rocky IV?

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.060
Oh, yeah. The Russian guy.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.640
Is that him? I think it's Dolph Lundgren.

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:15.720
He looks like that. He looks more like that with that.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.260
I can see him as a Terminator, you know?

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:20.160
Yeah, like in the next Terminator?

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.540
Yeah, this guy from the manual.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.440
It'll be like the drawing of this guy.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:25.620
Imagine him with the hair.

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:28.040
He kind of looks like, you know, like the robot version of him.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:28.880
Yeah, no, I can see it.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:33.680
Yeah. And then did you guys know that in Europe, this whole series for a very long time

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.180
was not called Contra?

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:40.040
Progressborg. Probotector is what it was called.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:41.500
I don't get it. Yeah.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:43.860
Probotector. Yeah.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.540
What is that? Like a Robocop?

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:46.840
Yeah, that's a...

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:47.900
I didn't hear that one.

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:48.760
But here's the weirdest thing.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:58.080
If you look at the box that they sold the Probotector ones in, if you look at Contra in the Japan version or the American version,

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.740
it's pretty much the same box art with Mad Dog and Scorpion on it.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:05.860
If you look at Probotector, it's two guys in mech suits.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:07.940
Yeah. That's weird.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:08.920
It's like Gundam.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.760
It's like you're selling that to the wrong audience, I feel like.

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:15.040
But there's also no mechs in the European version.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:19.280
They didn't add that, so it's like weird to just advertise that there's going to be something.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:26.660
Maybe they're just showcasing the one enemy in like level four that like has that turret thing.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.720
And that kind of looks like a mech that doesn't move.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.180
No, if you saw these guys on the box art, you would know.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:33.200
I know what I'm saying.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:34.500
You would know that like...

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.120
Maybe these are the things that they flew to the Amazon in and then said,

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.000
well, we don't need these to fight.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.100
I can just do it with my bare hands.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.800
The cover art is actually like the prologue to the game.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.060
So it's explaining how they got there.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:50.740
Exactly. Yeah, that's just how they flew in on their skells.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.480
Skells is like a Xenoblade cross thing.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:58.260
Skell? Yeah, that's like the mechs in Xenoblade cross are called skells.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:14:59.180
Enough with the weebs.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:05.320
Yeah, we're getting so into it that we might as well talk about the gameplay of Contra.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.140
Sean, you mentioned the graphics kind of blew you away in this one.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.000
Would you like to talk a little bit more in depth about that?

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:36.320
Sure. For the most part, I was pretty impressed with just the sprite work.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:41.200
Like I did mention the backgrounds, but the sprite work in general was what really got me

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.140
because like you can see the muscles in these guys.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:47.700
And I was just like, hell yeah.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:56.160
But later in the game, we didn't ever mention that it switches perspective like every once in a while.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:00.100
And you go into the pseudo 3D world.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:04.320
And even then, like that was pretty convincing.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:09.920
The last time I saw that perspective was like, what's the game?

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:11.200
Goonies 2?

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:17.120
Yeah. And it was much more primordial back then.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:20.220
It wasn't intuitive at all compared to this.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.380
And this, you're freely in control of your character.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:24.300
Except for the electric fences.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:31.540
Yeah. But to stay on track with the graphics there too, I think that the actual like stage designs were amazing too.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:40.520
If you look at, you know, Super Mario Brothers, you're sold on just like the bricks and like the occasional like little hill islands that they have.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:42.040
And the clouds and stuff.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:48.380
The same shading. Yeah, this has like full environments that sell me on the idea of like, I'm in the jungle.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:53.360
Now I'm in like these, I guess like bases during the pseudo 3D levels.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:17:01.180
The bases are a bit more convincing because the outdoor spaces still have some really weird geography that would only exist in a side-scrolling platformer game.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:05.680
That's true. There's a few like, not floating platforms, but a few islands of land.

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:07.660
But like it's still really good looking.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:08.800
Right. It works.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:17.080
Yeah, I think like there was never a point in the game where a design decision like wasn't correctly made for me.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.320
At least aesthetically. Yeah.

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:20.540
And I was immersed at all times.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.200
There wasn't a point where.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:28.980
Yeah. And to another point, is this the first time that we're seeing this like isometric 3D look?

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:31.400
Or has this happened before?

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:36.280
Because when I think of these games, you're looking at it straight on.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:45.080
You don't see any depth, but in Contra, you can actually see like, oh no, this platform extends beyond your body in the Z axis.

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:46.840
Did you catch this at all?

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:50.300
In the side-scrolling parts.

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:51.580
Oh, I thought you meant the...

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:54.400
No. Yeah. I think that that is a good example.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:18:01.640
I'm struggling to recall something, but I feel like, and maybe this is just my familiarity with 2D games, that I feel like we've seen that before.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.580
But it's done really convincingly here.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:14.700
Yeah. And there's, in the Famicom version, there actually is like certain extra details even added to the environments because they got like an extra chip added to theirs, which we didn't get in the NES version.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:19.460
But like the trees even like sway as if there's wind and stuff like that.

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:22.460
And the grass like kind of rustles and stuff.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:27.380
So I think like those extra bonuses would have been welcomed, but unsold on the other world.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:28.780
And I think that... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:38.980
I think going to what you were talking about with the level of detail on the Z axis, I think we've seen that only in games where it was pulled off poorly, the way I was talking about earlier, where...

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:40.460
Yeah, there's, I think in...

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:42.520
Was it Wizards and Warriors?

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.300
Yes. Where some of the platforms, like even though...

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:48.620
I think because of it, it made it hard to see where you're supposed to go.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:52.180
But we're trying to do that, and I think this is where they pulled it off.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:19:12.400
The last little bit about the graphics in this game, there are some projectile effects, specifically in some of the bosses, that are these weird little disks of energy that are probably the most detailed sprites that are just sort of like weird shapes that I've ever seen.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:15.000
It looks like it came out of a Super NES game.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.720
And I don't know if you know exactly what I'm talking about.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:18.780
They've got like a lighting to them.

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:20.620
Yeah, it's got a weird...

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:21.420
It looks really good.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:22.440
Even the bubbles do it really well.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:28.920
There's those blue transparent bubbles that are just, you know, in my mind, incredible to see this early on the NES.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:31.800
Yeah. Two other graphics thoughts, though.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:35.680
The way things explode is nice, too.

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:37.860
And it's not just kind of like a...

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:42.620
Sometimes with these games, you get the cop out of like when you kill the thing, it flashes and then just disappears.

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:44.280
I'm glad that we didn't get that.

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:45.800
It's a fiery explosion.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.520
Right, exactly. They really leaned into it, too.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.020
Even some of the walls explode.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.400
And as Sam mentioned earlier, too, it sounds good.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:58.120
When you're shooting your gun and when these explosions go off and stuff like that.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.820
You feel like you're in the chaos of it all.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:06.900
I was going to say that those two sections that are the, you know, on the other axis...

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.020
The pseudo 3D that stages two and four.

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:10.880
The 3D battles of World Runner sections.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.040
One thing I really liked about those is the...

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:21.100
Like when you go to a new area, you go through this dark spot first, you know, and then the lights come on and suddenly there's enemies there.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:22.340
Yeah. You know, it's just a nice little touch.

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:23.600
You're walking down that hallway, yeah.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:29.280
Yeah, you walk down a dark hallway, which I think is cool, you know, and there's nothing there and suddenly you're in the thick of it.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:35.120
Yeah. And you don't exactly know what's coming next, but you don't have to worry about that either.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:43.420
The game does give you, at least when you're playing single player for the most part, ample time to respond to things if you choose to lay back.

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:49.740
Yeah. But what I mean is there's like a little bit of pause in the action and you have to just kind of go into this dark space.

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:55.680
Oh, that's true too. They could have just loaded it up with the next section, you know, but instead it's like, no, you got to get ready.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.700
And it's your choice too to go forward.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:20:58.860
Yeah, I thought that was cool.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:09.380
One other graphics thing is that the progression from being like the Amazon to the alien lair, I thought was done well.

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:18.160
They could have maybe saved the surprise better in terms of how they lay out the enemies and bosses in the game because that's a little more random.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.040
It's like I didn't feel like I should.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:26.100
All of a sudden aliens just show up in stage three and I'm like the alien boss and he's a great boss, but I'm like.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.440
You're talking about the UFO? UFO? Not the UFO.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:30.840
I'm talking about the one with the swinging arms.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.260
Yeah. And that's like a fantastic sprite work on that guy.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:41.380
Yes. That's one of the bosses that I meant with the energy sprite thing.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:51.780
Yes. But also it just felt weird that, you know, they took such a careful design in how the stages would progress from like normal Amazon to alien.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:59.440
But when the like actual enemy placement and stuff like that, it's just like, I think now is a good time to introduce aliens into the world.

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:00.840
Like you're just climbing a hill.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:06.000
Yeah. Well, because otherwise, if you hadn't read the manual and you get to those aliens at the end, other than those bosses, you'd be like, what the heck?

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:07.620
I thought I was fighting people all this time.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:09.320
But do you think you expect that?

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:11.180
Like, I mean, in 1980s.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:12.920
I guess because of what video games were.

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:15.060
Yeah, exactly. In 1980s, we were all stupid.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:18.700
It was probably just like, oh, yeah, of course there's aliens in this game.

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:20.260
Like, I'm shocked if there's not.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:26.100
Yeah, right. And now let's talk about the platforming for the game.

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:30.600
You know, and this can include jumping and controls in general.

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:39.580
But, you know, the game, while we described it as a run and gun because you're mostly dealing with firepower and taking down enemies, there is a fair bit of platforming.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:41.580
And later on, certain stages have puzzles.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:44.120
So how did we feel about movement in the game in general?

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:48.100
I thought that it was movement was pretty tight.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:56.500
The only thing I didn't like was that in order to go down from the top of a platform, you have to press A, like an anti-jump kind of.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:02.380
And that would mess me up sometimes when I had to do, like, quick movements, like quick jump down, dodge something, and shoot.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:05.040
I'm funny. I think that's funny that you say that.

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:08.000
It's a problem because I thought that felt fairly intuitive to me.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:15.940
Yeah. I guess the only other thing you could have done, for those of you that don't know, you have to actually, like, lay down when you hold the down on the D-pad, your player character.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:19.480
You go prime. Like, yeah, they go military on the floor.

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:22.460
I go military on the floor.

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:23.480
Military pose, you know.

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:24.820
Army crawl. Yeah.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:27.380
Army crawl. They just call it crawl, though.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:29.880
Yeah. They don't crawl, right?

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:31.340
You can't move them.

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:33.480
If you're holding the D-pad down, you can't.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.180
Like, they'll go back to walking if you move, right?

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:42.800
That's a weird thing. But anyway, once they're down on the ground like that, if you hit your jump button, they'll be able to go to the ledge below them.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:48.540
I guess the only other thing you could have done is maybe, like, double tap down on the D-pad to go down.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.660
But I could see that actually leading to, like, more awkward unintentional jumping.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:56.100
Especially because you can shoot down by holding down.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:59.920
So I think that would give you the problem where you'd be trying to shoot down and you'd accidentally go through.

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:04.600
And moving through ledges like that, like, when you want to go down to a ledge.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:05.880
It doesn't happen that often.

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:12.320
So I kind of like that it wasn't, like, a button that you would be doing often.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:20.880
Yeah. I actually, to be honest, didn't know about – I didn't know there was a way to do that from platform to platform until, like, much later in the game when I think you needed to do it.

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:24.060
In the energy zone, I think, even, which is, like, what, level 5?

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:25.880
Huh. But then you needed to do it.

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:27.580
And I was like, wow, this would have been really helpful earlier.

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:30.740
But I also – that's just from me not having read the manual first.

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.540
You know, I would have thought you would have done it on the very first boss.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:35.580
Well, maybe if I had known.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:36.980
Yeah. I would have done it, yeah.

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:45.580
Like, the very first boss kind of sets it up as, like, a – you want to take advantage of being able to quickly access the floors below you.

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:49.400
But I never thought of – I never thought of how to do that.

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:51.960
I just assumed you couldn't do it because I was trying to press down.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:52.780
Well, you've got to read the manual sometimes.

00:24:52.780 --> 00:25:03.440
Exactly. And then, I don't know about you guys, but for me, the jump is great for its range, but I hated that it was a complete jump every time.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:14.520
Yeah. That was a problem because sometimes the level is arranged in such a way that there is an enemy just shooting bullets over your head and you would never get hit by it unless you have to jump.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:15.980
And you have to jump.

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.080
And you just jump right into the bullet.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:28.160
And while it has, like, you're always at max range, that max range is always just a little too short to get to certain platforms.

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:32.060
And these certain platforms look like you should be able to reach them.

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:43.120
So, the jump, while it – at least you have some control while you're in midair, it's a little – it has enough flaws that I can get annoyed by it.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:50.160
Right. It's great that you can completely move left to right as needed because sometimes that's the whole reason you're jumping to dodge bullets in the first place.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:57.140
Mm-hmm. But I hate that you couldn't, like, tap A to get a slightly less higher –

00:25:57.140 --> 00:26:00.080
Right. Even if it was just, like, maybe two to three stages.

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:02.860
It didn't have to be, like, as in-depth as Mario's.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:12.460
Yeah. But I just – I think that, you know, there's so many points that you run into in this game, especially with the bosses, where a complete jump actually hangs you up for a while.

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:15.780
Yeah. And then you have to kind of now think about, like, okay, I'm in the air for this long.

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:16.940
Like, where do I land?

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:18.180
Yeah, right. That's safe, you know?

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:29.820
Yeah. It is nice, though, that you can – because I know some games don't allow this – that you can still fire while you're in the air because that sometimes is, like, a huge detriment when you jump and you're just stuck doing nothing.

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:32.380
Yeah, and it's very important. You have to do that at certain points.

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:43.060
And directional shooting is new to us, especially in this way where you can hold up and left to shoot, you know, in that direction.

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:49.940
And so I wonder, having this be the first time, did you guys feel like they pulled it off right away?

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:51.400
Yeah, they definitely pulled it off.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:57.480
I do have one minor gripe about it, although I can't think of a good solution to it.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:03.620
But there are a lot of times I'm trying to shoot diagonally up but not run forward because there's nothing you can really do about it.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.940
I understand that. I think that's just a part of the flow of the game that you have to get used to.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:13.840
Right, yeah. But thinking of, like, the way you can stop just below something and shoot straight up and you can shoot straight down.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.300
And I know that the limitations of having a tiny controller –

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:17.660
You do have to jump to shake. But I wish there was a shake.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:18.740
It would straight down, though. Yeah.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:20.040
Like, if you were – Yeah, that's true.

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:21.820
I don't even know how you do it.

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:23.580
Like, if you hold A or something after a jump –

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:26.760
It would have to be this mess of button combinations to do that.

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:30.680
I agree it would probably make it worse with the NES controller.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.640
I just – that was one thing that, you know, I got hung up on a few times.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:39.780
One spot where that really got me was when that one – the armored truck comes at you.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:42.120
Yeah. In, like, the third level or something.

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:55.640
And, like, the best way to do it is to, like, go all the way to the left side of the screen and crouch down or, you know, crawl and then shoot diagonally up or shoot –

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:57.720
Right. No, you can actually –

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:02.060
You can shoot – It's misleading, but you can actually shoot straight on even though there's, like, a giant shield there.

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:04.200
Yeah. Hitting any part of that tank is damage.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:06.800
Oh, okay. Yeah, and they give you the confirmation sound for that.

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.520
So it's not like you just, like, find that out on accident.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:13.300
Yeah. But that's an interesting strategy on your part, too, is to come up with that.

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:19.860
But, like, turning around also at that point when you have to go to the edge of the screen and then turn around really quickly but you don't want to move that far.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:23.140
I don't know. But, I mean, again, what can you do?

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:24.220
I think I actually mentioned that.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:26.860
I was playing Chris, our audio producer, was in the room.

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:33.260
And there were a few times where I was like I needed to be on this pixel but then I needed to turn around.

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:38.560
And to turn in that direction, you would move a step in that direction no matter what.

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:54.180
Right. And I found that when you shoot down diagonally, it's a lot harder to be precise whereas when I was shooting, you know, in the upper diagonals, you know, walking wasn't like – I felt like that was the natural way to move things.

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:55.220
So it was, like, fine.

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:59.380
But when I wanted to shoot down below, a lot of times I would wind up crouching instead.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:02.280
And I wasn't – sometimes that's not great.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.080
Yeah, the controls aren't perfect.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:20.580
And one thing that – I don't know if it's just because of my exposure to later games but I feel like the crouch slash prone button is a little too touchy because I would – and maybe this is my own fault.

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:42.600
And just I haven't – I'm not holding the button down correctly but I felt like when I was crouching to avoid projectiles, if I let off that button for, like, just one little, like, sliver of a pound, I would just stand right up into fire.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:52.460
And I don't know if it's because, like, later games have a bit of an animation of you standing up and it's not immediate so that you have that little cushion.

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:57.920
But I found myself making that mistake over and over even after yelling at myself to stop.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.760
And I don't know if we had anything similar.

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:07.420
I only had that, but, like, repeatedly in the forward-facing levels and the pseudo-3D levels.

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:09.380
Yeah. But, yeah, I definitely had the same thing.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:24.920
Just because we're about to get into the pseudo-3D thing, I did want to just mention real fast because this is kind of funny to me that the first boss of the game before you get to the pseudo-3D stage is this, like, wall that has, like, a guy on the top shooting down at you.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:26.340
There's these two turrets.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:31.820
And then there's just, like, this icon that you have to shoot that's, like, the keyhole, I guess, or whatever.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:38.020
Yeah. But what I like about it is it's, like, kind of an ode to Gradius of, like, the shoot-the-core thing.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:49.240
Yeah. And it's, like, even in a non-traditional shmup like Contra where you're actually running gun, you still have to shoot the core on a lot of these things to get through to the next level.

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:57.660
Yeah. I didn't actually see that as a—I mean, this is just however you perceive a boss as—but I didn't actually see that as a boss, thought more as, like, just a set piece.

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:04.620
Interesting. I guess I just always see in these games the end of a stage is just, like, always has a boss for me.

00:31:04.780 --> 00:31:05.800
Like, that's who the boss is.

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:07.080
But you're probably more right.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:14.700
It is more like a—and this is why the next part of the level is, like, 3D because you went into the base.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:22.220
Which is fine. So let's talk about the base because I think that's something really cool that they did pretty well by my book.

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:29.640
Yeah. They made the screen, if you guys can tell, they put, like, a border around it so they actually didn't have to animate as many pixels.

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:33.660
Mm-hmm. They put, like, a cave border around it.

00:31:33.940 --> 00:31:41.960
And that's something that you'll see later NES games like Kirby's Adventure do really well is that the whole game actually just has a border around it.

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:47.760
So they can put more pixels and animation into the actual screen design.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:50.200
Doom wound up doing that even on the Super Nintendo.

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:52.900
That was a—that was a—to solve a problem.

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:55.900
Right, right. But I think, like, you know, same—you could say the same thing here.

00:31:56.020 --> 00:32:00.340
It's solving a problem because who's to say that if you don't have that cave border that it's going to—

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:05.760
Work all that well. Work as well as it did or flow as nice because there's a lot of bullets on the screen.

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:13.200
Yeah, these are very busy stages where it's sort of like a—it's sort of like a tennis match but only—

00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:15.020
I don't know why I say that.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:17.960
You know what? It's funny because I don't know why you say that either but it makes sense.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:19.040
It does make sense. I get it.

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:23.940
A tennis match if the idea is that you should be dodging the balls that your opponent shoots.

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:28.240
I feel like there's a tennis game that's in this perspective or something.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:29.940
I mean, there's a tennis game that we play.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.080
There's, like, the net of the electricity.

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:36.740
In tennis, they call it, like, a volley, which is also, like, I think—

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:37.980
You can refer to that.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.640
Like, weapons. Weapons is, like, firing volleys.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:44.260
Yeah. It's basically just tennis.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:46.720
This is my favorite sports game so far on the NES.

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:48.300
Yeah. Conjure's a sports game.

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:49.340
Who knew? I like it.

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:54.600
And there's, like, a lot of targets in this stage that you have to shoot and take down.

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:58.280
And to be clear, too—I don't know if we've actually said this yet, but this is a—from

00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:02.180
the perspective behind your character, behind whatever his name is, the Scorpion or whatever

00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:03.080
his name is. Behind Scorpion.

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:06.620
And he just moves side to side and shoots forward.

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:08.640
So it's like—it feels 3D.

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:10.740
I don't know if we were clear on it yet.

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:13.340
I guess it could have had some more clarification.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:17.080
Yeah, and it plays more like a 3D world runner from that kind of—

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:18.180
I don't like that.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.980
I think that is a discredit to Contra.

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:27.080
Absolutely. There's no goofy, like, fake type R—type 7?

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:28.860
What was the name of the thing? Yeah, mode 7.

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:34.200
Mode 7. There's no, like, fake 3D movement going on in terms of the actual stage.

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:40.940
It's just—if you're on the left side of the screen, you're going to be firing at the

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:42.760
left side of the far wall.

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:45.580
And vice versa.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:50.760
And there's also—like, crouching works the same, except it's going to shoot the bottom

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:55.040
of the far wall. It's pretty intuitive once you get used to it.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:56.560
So I guess that isn't intuitive.

00:33:57.960 --> 00:34:04.320
It's fine. I think that what I meant by that is just from, like, how it looks perspective-wise of how

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:05.340
you control the player.

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:11.160
But on top of that, this mode made me feel a lot more defensive, whereas I was saying

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:13.340
that the other one, you constantly want to be shooting and everything like that.

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:20.120
In this one, I was ducking for a majority of the level and then waiting for my opportunity

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:25.420
to attack. Well, because you don't have any motivation or even the ability to move forward until

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:27.480
you've cleared this one room.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:29.520
Right, there's that electric fence in the way.

00:34:29.800 --> 00:34:33.860
Whereas in every other—like, the side-scrolling parts of this game, you have to constantly be

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:36.360
moving. You'll be fighting the same guys over and over.

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:39.860
It does feel a little bit like a cover-based shooting game at this point.

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:45.660
You crouch behind the electric fence and it protects you, and you've got to jump up to—or

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:49.640
pop up to shoot things that are either at your eye level or sometimes you have to jump

00:34:49.640 --> 00:34:50.580
to shoot things that are higher.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:54.800
But then, like, constantly watching where the bullets are and ducking when they come

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:59.920
near you. It's a pretty, like, different way of playing than the first level.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.520
It's interesting. And then they have those rolling—whatever they are, the things that roll on the floor

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:06.540
at you? Are they—

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:07.560
Yeah. Explosive—

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.880
It might be like TNT, right?

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:12.280
Yeah. Something. But they come at you in different patterns, which I think is cool.

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:16.160
So you can—because for me, if it was just coming straight at me, it was kind of hard

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:16.980
at the time to jump.

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:19.720
And I found myself landing on it a lot.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:23.340
Yeah. The— Yeah, the depth perception in this game isn't perfect.

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:25.100
I stopped jumping over those at all.

00:35:25.240 --> 00:35:26.040
Yeah. I just started shooting them.

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:27.640
Yeah. I didn't realize that you could shoot them.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:30.280
Exactly. Then I started shooting them, and I was like, oh!

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:31.280
Oh, yeah, of course.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:36.000
It didn't occur to me until I played with you in co-op, and when I saw you shoot at them,

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:38.480
I was like, why didn't I ever think that I could shoot these things?

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:42.360
Yeah. There's something psychological about them that makes them look indestructible.

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:46.160
Yeah. Because I think the patterns make it look like you're supposed to be jumping over those.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:51.320
Right. But also, jumping to the—if they're in, like, a V pattern, and one of them is coming

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:55.680
at you, like, the first one's coming at you, or the—I don't know, you can kind

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:57.640
of jump over them sideways, too.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:35:58.880
Yeah. That makes it a little bit easier.

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:00.380
That's the only other thing you could do, right?

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:01.800
Yeah. Yeah, I don't know.

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:03.280
It's like a weird little mix-up.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:09.760
Yeah. It's cool. Well, there's also a weird—if you are doing the jumping strategy, there is a weird perspective

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:14.380
to it where I felt like a lot of times when I was trying to do the jump, I was jumping

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:18.740
way too early, and it would still be on the screen and get me at the end of it.

00:36:18.840 --> 00:36:20.000
That's why this isn't perfect.

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:24.840
Right. And you kind of have to, like, assess, like, you know, this is a different kind of 3D,

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:29.040
so I've got to wait until these things are really close to me before I jump because they're

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:30.840
going to stay on the screen for a while.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:00.820
Mm-hmm.

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:03.240
Like, the vertical scrolling game example of choice?

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:05.300
See, I thought this was more of, like, a flat-wave scrolling.

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:06.500
Flat-wave scrolling.

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:10.780
Great. Either way, all you need to know is that instead of moving left to right, in this one you're,

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:18.240
like, kind of climbing up a hill via platforms that include such things as floating, like,

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:20.940
rock things that, like, no one questions, right?

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:21.980
It's like, at this point—

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:22.840
Just getting out of the side.

00:37:22.940 --> 00:37:27.300
That's how I see it. Right. I mean, it looks like the kind of stuff that Sonic the Hedgehog has to deal with.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:29.820
Not necessarily the scorpion in Mad Dog.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:31.880
It's just scorpion.

00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:33.680
Oh, it's not the scorpion?

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:36.660
Oh, it's the amazing Spider-Man villain?

00:37:37.000 --> 00:37:42.960
Yeah. There's also these—because you're climbing up a waterfall, and there are these, like,

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:46.500
people with, like, scuba gear in the waterfall.

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:47.760
They're, like, a snorkel.

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:48.920
They pop out of the waterfall.

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:52.700
They shoot just little pools, like, along the waterfall.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:53.760
I never thought those were—

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:56.800
No, it's the waterfall. I never thought those were people, though, because you can't shoot at them.

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:57.960
You can't. Yeah, they're underwater.

00:37:58.020 --> 00:37:59.460
You can't shoot at somebody underwater.

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:00.560
Is this a power-up?

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:01.640
Is this a—what is this? Right.

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:04.000
I agree. I did try to pick that up every time.

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:05.240
They flop up and shoot at you, though.

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:06.620
They do. Yes. Yeah.

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:08.160
Once you go in front of them.

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:09.400
Yeah. I thought it was only over them.

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:10.540
Yeah, yeah. That's what I mean.

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:13.640
It's, like, when you—if you're behind them, you just don't know what—you know,

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:14.820
that's how you see them first.

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:19.200
Because you're scrolling up the stage, when you first see them, you're not sure what that

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:20.500
is. You shoot at it.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:22.560
It doesn't do anything. You jump at it.

00:38:22.580 --> 00:38:26.760
It doesn't do anything. So, finally, once you pass it, then it's, like, I'm going to kill you.

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:29.560
Well, they are phenomenal swimmers.

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:34.560
I'll just say that. I'm looking at a video right now, and it looks like after you're on top of them, you can fire

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:35.800
down on them and shoot them.

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:37.300
No way. Okay.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:39.360
Yeah. That makes sense, though, because they're out of the water.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:46.400
Yeah. They shoot at you. Do we know what mechanism is happening on the bridge with the fire that goes back and

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:47.320
forth? Oh, yeah.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:48.600
Like, is that just, like—

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:50.840
It's a fire bridge. Just a fire bridge.

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:54.300
Yeah. And what makes that different from the fire that happened on the other two bridges in

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:57.040
stage one that completely collapsed those bridges?

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:59.420
No, that's just the dynamite blowing them up.

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:00.920
That's detonation, yeah. There was dynamite?

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:03.860
Well, I mean, like, it's tiny dynamite.

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:06.840
They had it rigged to blow in case you guys came.

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:10.060
You guys are so immersed in this game, you're seeing things that aren't even in it.

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:13.300
No, but I didn't—all right, I wouldn't say specifically dynamite, but I definitely thought

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:14.920
that was, like, a detonated explosion.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:16.000
Okay, fine. It was C4. Okay.

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:20.340
And is it possible, or is this, like, just, like, you know, recessed rumors?

00:39:20.620 --> 00:39:24.620
Is it possible to, like, not fall into the water on that stage one?

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:27.300
Yeah, you have to jump. You jump and hop it.

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:31.220
Yeah, you have to just time the jump to hit the one that you know is going to.

00:39:31.220 --> 00:39:32.080
I always fell in the water. Yeah, me too.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:34.360
You have to jump to the second, like, or the third.

00:39:34.360 --> 00:39:36.140
There's no penalty for falling in the water.

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:37.340
In fact, it's probably more interesting.

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:41.860
Yeah, I actually, after I, like, learned you can jump over it, like, next time I played

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:43.320
it, I just was like, I'm just going to go in the water.

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:47.100
I kind of want to talk about that for a second, actually, because that made me realize that

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.340
one of my very first impressions of this game when I was playing it—

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:57.020
Callback. Yeah, was that once the bridge exploded and I missed it, I expected to die.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:02.860
I expected, like, that I would either respawn or get, like, a, you know, back to the high

00:40:02.860 --> 00:40:05.280
score screen and then start the level over.

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:10.000
I was shocked that I, like, landed in the water and could now swim, and I was like, oh,

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:14.200
it's going to be a good game. It's kind of like a learning section.

00:40:14.560 --> 00:40:17.360
Yeah, yeah, yeah. It's like, hey, by the way, you can be in the water.

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:21.700
It does burn itself in the butt, though, when there is a level later in the game where

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.300
you can fall in water and then you die.

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:26.380
Yeah, that's true. It's teaching you the wrong thing sometimes.

00:40:27.020 --> 00:40:32.200
I know Sean's been dying to talk about the power-ups in the game, and I like them, too,

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:37.080
because this, again, kind of enforces the idea of, like, a light shmup that we have on our

00:40:37.080 --> 00:40:41.520
hands here. So, Sean, you want to take us through, like, a little bit of a power-up walkthrough?

00:40:41.800 --> 00:40:47.720
I'd love to. I don't know where you got the idea that I really wanted to talk about them, but I will.

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:50.680
There are several upgrades.

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:57.960
There's the machine gun, which now, instead of, like, this single semi-automatic burst

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:02.240
shot that you got, you can just hold it down, and you can go about your business.

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:10.900
Then there's laser, which I find to be terrible, because when you fire it twice in succession,

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:12.800
I think it deletes the first shot.

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:13.780
Which doesn't make any sense.

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:14.680
I don't understand it.

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:16.600
Cancel out your original laser.

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:19.620
I don't know if that's just because, like, you can't have so many sprites on the same

00:41:19.620 --> 00:41:20.800
scan. I don't know.

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:25.800
Then you got the fire, I guess?

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:26.980
Yeah, fireball.

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:32.280
Fireball, and that just does the loop-de-loop thing that we've been seeing in a few games

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:33.140
lately. Yeah.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:34.800
I like it.

00:41:34.940 --> 00:41:37.820
I don't like it as much as spread.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:40.080
Spread's the best. Yeah.

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:43.840
You can totally cheese bosses with spread.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:49.400
It's not going to, like, make this an auto-win, but this is the best power-up.

00:41:50.720 --> 00:41:53.580
And then there's, like, an invincibility one.

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:55.480
I think it's a B, I think.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:58.780
It has the B. In the manual, it refers to it as force field.

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.020
Yeah. But that one was very rare.

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:03.480
B is from beast mode. It was rare for that to show up for me.

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:05.180
I don't know about you guys. I got it maybe twice.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:12.240
I think that one at least shows up in predetermined points.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:14.380
But that's just my assumption.

00:42:14.620 --> 00:42:18.660
Okay. That would make sense, because I could see that being kind of like op if it shows up right

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:20.520
before a boss or something like that.

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:24.920
And then there's an R, but I don't know what R does.

00:42:25.060 --> 00:42:27.560
R is the rapid-fire one, which just shoots it.

00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:32.540
It's different than the machine gun in the sense that I believe, and don't quote me on

00:42:32.540 --> 00:42:37.400
this because it might have just been me feeling it, like a pseudo thing.

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:41.360
If you have the spread gun and then get the R...

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:42.380
Oh, no, it's a rapid spread.

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:45.120
It will shoot the spread gun faster.

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:48.160
I did not know that it was, like, an attachment.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:49.240
I didn't realize that either.

00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:55.200
It felt like to me it stacked because when you get the rapid-fire Falcon, it doesn't change

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:56.640
to a different thing.

00:42:56.660 --> 00:43:00.760
If you had the spread gun and you pick up the R, you don't lose the spread gun.

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:03.680
So I assumed it was just making the spread gun shoot more frequently.

00:43:03.980 --> 00:43:07.620
Yeah. And these things are just sort of either hidden around the level...

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:12.120
Not hidden, but stored around the level in these opening and closing door things.

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:13.700
Called pillbox pensors.

00:43:14.040 --> 00:43:15.980
No, I think those are the floating things, right?

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:17.140
No, that's the flying capsules.

00:43:17.140 --> 00:43:23.560
Oh, gotcha, gotcha. And sometimes they will come across the screen just to give you a quick boost.

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:26.740
A welcome sight, but kind of confused with an enemy.

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:28.520
Yeah, I thought it was an enemy for a while.

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:33.360
I shot it down a lot, and even though I saw the power come out of it...

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:36.520
I still thought it was an enemy. This game is very reactionary.

00:43:37.100 --> 00:43:38.180
What you see on the screen...

00:43:38.180 --> 00:43:39.060
It's all lizard brain.

00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:40.120
Yeah, you want to kill it.

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:43.040
No matter what it is on the screen, you want to take it down.

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:45.860
So pretty much if I saw something...

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:48.540
I'm confident I shot it trees a lot.

00:43:49.640 --> 00:43:56.240
You know, there's that part in stage four, the snow level, where the bombs are being...

00:43:56.240 --> 00:43:58.080
The trees start throwing grenades at you.

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:00.740
Yeah, well, Sean thinks that the trees are throwing the grenades.

00:44:01.100 --> 00:44:04.700
I think that there's people behind the trees that we can't...

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:05.780
All right, Mike, you're talking about...

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:10.080
Okay, so you're so immersed in this game that you think that things exist when you don't even see them.

00:44:10.180 --> 00:44:13.140
I thought that they were below our horizon line.

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:16.340
Yeah, okay. Because sometimes there's no trees, but there's still...

00:44:16.340 --> 00:44:17.820
There's a little cliff or a little hill there.

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:19.520
That's true. And they're below there throwing them at you.

00:44:19.560 --> 00:44:20.440
We can't look down there, though.

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:25.200
And, you know, that's frustrating because you want to kill whatever's throwing those things,

00:44:25.220 --> 00:44:27.940
and now you've been introduced to non-killable enemies.

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:31.460
It's insane. Something that's just going to try and ruin your life.

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:33.060
Forever. Yeah.

00:44:33.860 --> 00:44:39.240
But one thing we did talk about that was exclusive to the NES version is the Mass Destruction Falcon,

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:44.220
which is a very rare power that shows up that destroys everything on screen.

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:46.920
I actually might have gotten that once.

00:44:46.980 --> 00:44:48.500
It just happens right away, right?

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:50.640
Yeah. As soon as you pick it up, it just happens.

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:53.440
I think I got it once at a very, like, inopportune moment.

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:54.500
There were, like, two things on screen.

00:44:54.620 --> 00:44:55.940
I remember just seeing them disappear.

00:44:55.940 --> 00:45:00.940
Well, it's not in the arcade version, so I do wonder if you're going to include it in the NES version,

00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:03.740
why not, like, why not make it show up more?

00:45:03.920 --> 00:45:06.540
Like, why make it such a rare thing that you won't even notice it?

00:45:07.180 --> 00:45:15.800
I don't know. I think if it was more common, then it would just be a, it would be unbalanced.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:20.260
I'm talking more common than, like, twice in the whole game because here's the thing.

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:25.040
Like Joe said, it happens as, you can't store it, so it happens as soon as you pick it up.

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:28.980
So a lot of times that's just, like, maybe, like, two henchmen get destroyed.

00:45:29.080 --> 00:45:29.980
Yeah, whoever happens to be on the screen.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.980
Yeah, and they might run right back into screen.

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:37.240
Yeah. There are enough enemies that I feel like this happening a few times more wouldn't be like,

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:38.540
oh, wow, this game's so easy now.

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:39.900
It would just be, yeah.

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:45.540
I think that there really isn't a perfect solution, so I think they should have just gotten rid of it.

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:46.780
Okay. Yeah, I think that's fair.

00:45:46.860 --> 00:45:47.740
It wasn't in the arcade one.

00:45:47.820 --> 00:45:49.020
It's kind of unnecessary here.

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:52.140
Yeah. Were there any power-ups that we would have liked to see?

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:57.360
I wanted to see that Mega Man electric burst.

00:45:57.700 --> 00:46:00.220
Okay. But that's just because I thought it would look cool.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:06.540
Gotcha. I was thinking also, you know how, like, in Gradius you have your option, which is, like,

00:46:06.640 --> 00:46:10.580
a ball that kind of, like, protects you before you die?

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:13.880
I would have liked something like that that was different than Force Field because Force

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:16.440
Field's more like the star in Super Mario Bros.

00:46:16.520 --> 00:46:17.940
where you're just invincible for a little bit.

00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:23.340
But an option to have almost, like, a shield, you know, where, like, oh, you can take one

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:25.100
hit and you won't die.

00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:27.400
Yeah. And then you'll, you know, like, that would have been nice.

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:33.260
That would have been nice. The Force Field 2 would have been a little bit better if there was any indication of

00:46:33.260 --> 00:46:34.240
when it was going to end.

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:37.560
Yeah. Right. Yeah. There's no music change, right?

00:46:37.560 --> 00:46:39.660
There's no music change. You're not going to hear the da-da-da-da.

00:46:39.920 --> 00:46:43.120
Yeah. It's just you're playing Contra, but you're flashing.

00:46:43.600 --> 00:46:46.340
Right. And that's not entirely helpful.

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:47.420
No. No, is it? No.

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:52.660
And then one thing I wanted to mention about the power-ups, and this is me just asking

00:46:52.660 --> 00:46:57.000
for way too much. I'm not saying it's a flaw in the game, but I was kind of expecting, because of how good

00:46:57.000 --> 00:47:01.280
the graphics are, that when you pick up different weapons that your gun would change a little.

00:47:01.880 --> 00:47:05.700
Yeah. You know, like, if you have the machine gun, you would at least, like, look like you're

00:47:05.700 --> 00:47:08.300
holding some kind of machine gun or, like, Gatling gun.

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:13.160
Like, that would be cool. You know, but, like, that's just wishful thinking on my part.

00:47:13.260 --> 00:47:15.720
It's not an actual. I'm not detracting any points from Contra.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:17.860
Right. Not having that would have just been a nice touch.

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:48.200
Yeah. We'll see you next time.

00:47:56.340 --> 00:47:59.140
We haven't really talked about difficulty in the game.

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:04.680
We've kind of said like, oh, you know, that boss might have been a little cheap or like, you know, certain enemies spawn in weird places.

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:08.540
But how do we feel overall about the difficulty of Contra?

00:48:08.540 --> 00:48:18.440
This is something I was surprised about, too, because I always this is like almost has this legendary status of being the Nintendo hard game, at least in my head.

00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:20.240
That's how that's what I thought the trope was.

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:23.220
Maybe that's why I didn't think it was quite as bad.

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:26.680
But it's a very learnable, playable game.

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:27.760
I mean, it's challenging.

00:48:27.800 --> 00:48:30.740
Trust me. Like, it's definitely it's definitely tough, but it's not.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:32.760
It's not impossible.

00:48:33.320 --> 00:48:36.620
We are men of the Ghost and Goblins generation.

00:48:37.140 --> 00:48:38.600
We played that game.

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:42.080
We've seen what what really hard games are like.

00:48:42.260 --> 00:48:44.640
I don't think this is on that scale of it.

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:53.560
And I also think that we've played a lot of bad games that are only hard because of how bad they are in terms of like how you control your character.

00:48:53.840 --> 00:48:57.880
I think Contra meets it right in the middle, but it's still a very difficult game.

00:48:58.240 --> 00:48:59.660
Yeah, it is a difficult game. Yeah, don't misunderstand me.

00:49:00.100 --> 00:49:04.300
It's definitely hard, but it's hard in the way that I feel attention while I'm playing.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:06.440
That's tension, not attention.

00:49:06.760 --> 00:49:14.220
I feel tension while I'm playing because I know I can do it, because I know I can dodge all these little things, rather than just frustration.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:15.520
I think that there is.

00:49:15.620 --> 00:49:20.360
However, I do think that there are a lot of points in this game that the hardness is just frustration.

00:49:20.420 --> 00:49:28.140
Like, I don't know what level it is, but before the show, we were we were playing through it.

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:42.360
And Mike and I were going through a sequence where there were these guys with like the the turrets and they would just pop up right in front of you with a gun with a bullet already shooting.

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:47.760
And you had to just have a split that literal split second to get down.

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:52.920
And while I think, yeah, this is going to test your reflexes, I think that that's just a little much.

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:55.500
Yeah, it's the energy zone in the hangar zone.

00:49:55.580 --> 00:50:01.780
And to be fair, they're late stages, but there's a lot of things being introduced there that are a little crazy.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:03.740
That's also the same stages that we get the flamethrower.

00:50:03.740 --> 00:50:12.100
Yeah. And the flamethrower, it does have a puzzle element to it, but only in the sections where it's by itself.

00:50:12.100 --> 00:50:19.800
When you have to deal with the flamethrower and the normal minions and the guys with the machine guns, that's a lot to take care of on any particular stage.

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:25.320
And I think that that's where, you know, pure gamers, close your ears.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:31.980
I think that's where this game really finds its strength in having more than three lives.

00:50:32.420 --> 00:50:36.520
Okay. Yeah. Three lives is, you know, and I know people can do it.

00:50:36.620 --> 00:50:40.380
Don't get me wrong. If you've played this game since it came out, you're probably pretty good at it.

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:52.240
But for the most of us who have grown up with easier games, when we finally played this game with the Konami code and had the 30 lives, I actually felt like I was getting more out of the game with 30 lives.

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:56.560
And yes, the respawn on the same place where you died is a little cheap.

00:50:56.720 --> 00:51:01.160
I don't know if I, you know, you could have pushed us back a little, but I don't know how they would program that.

00:51:01.160 --> 00:51:13.380
But that's a little cheap, but I think overall having even, I want to say, unlimited lives, like, makes the game more fun because it's still fun to play this game despite how difficult it is.

00:51:13.460 --> 00:51:22.500
I'm not saying that every game would be better if you had 99 lives, but this game would have benefited from something like that because I just really wanted to keep playing through it.

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:25.520
I kind of hated when you ran out of all your lives and had to restart.

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:36.140
Yeah, I agree, and it's interesting to me that I agree so much because I'm usually pretty, like, when you want the full experience of a game, I don't like that, even if it means I'm not going to beat the game.

00:51:36.280 --> 00:51:46.120
But in this case, I think that it works better only because this has a flaw, what I consider a flaw, that it's just a personal preference.

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:53.760
I just don't like when games give you three lives, you continue, three lives, you continue, three lives, and then you have to start the whole game over again.

00:51:53.820 --> 00:51:55.280
Right, you only have to recontill it.

00:51:55.520 --> 00:52:00.340
And then I understand, like, I'm all for, like, a level of, like, punishment when you lose to make it more worth it.

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:03.100
Go back to the beginning of the level, whatever you want it to be.

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:15.760
But having to start the whole game over again, I think for just what we're used to is what makes, for me, it just worth it to put in the Konami code and play with those 30 lives, which might be a little much.

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:26.600
But I think that this game needs to have some limit to the amount of lives that you have because otherwise the game is just an exercise in attrition.

00:52:26.980 --> 00:52:32.960
And it's just you, how many times do you die until you reach the end, not can you reach the end?

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:40.600
Because if it's just 99 lives or 100 or infinite lives until the end of the game, it's just like, well, I'm going to finish this game within the hour.

00:52:40.980 --> 00:52:42.800
It's just, I'm just probably going to die a bunch of times.

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:44.120
I agree.

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:46.440
I think there could have been a happy medium.

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:52.300
If the Konami code made it so you have unlimited continues but still three lives, I think that would have been better.

00:52:52.300 --> 00:52:56.780
Where after your three lives, you go back to the beginning of the stage, not the beginning of the entire game.

00:52:57.020 --> 00:53:00.500
Well, I mean, when it comes to cheats, I think anything goes with that.

00:53:00.700 --> 00:53:05.820
But I'm just saying for making it a better experience, if we're not considering it of a cheat, if it's just like, this is just another way to play the game.

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:17.820
I think what Joe's saying is game design-wise, if after your third continue, if you still just got to go back to the beginning of the current level instead of starting all the way back at level one, that would have been a better design.

00:53:17.820 --> 00:53:22.340
Well, then just get rid of continues and just have it be like you go back to the beginning of the stage.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:25.940
Why not even die? Yeah, every time you die, you go back to the beginning of the stage.

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:27.620
I think that's the best way to do it.

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:31.460
Yeah, I should. Honestly, where's Yoshi with wings in Contra?

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:34.860
Don't get me started. I'm replaying Final Fantasy VIII.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:36.440
They've got that option. You just can't die.

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:37.540
And I hate that it's there.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:38.540
I absolutely hate it.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:40.240
I know. Even though I can just not touch it.

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:41.820
And I don't. But I hate that it's there.

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:47.580
What's crazy about the continues thing, I just want to bring this up because I read this right before we went on the podcast.

00:53:48.060 --> 00:53:55.540
If you're playing the arcade version, you can't even pay your way through that three continues and you're out thing.

00:53:55.820 --> 00:54:02.120
Wow. Even on the third continue, it will take you back to the very first stage if you choose to give it more money.

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:07.000
Yeah, if you ask the machine, like, you please let me do this for $5.

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:08.960
Right. You know, they usually give you, like, a timer.

00:54:09.140 --> 00:54:12.200
Like, you have 30 seconds to put in 25 more sets to play Contra.

00:54:12.200 --> 00:54:17.460
It's like, even on your third set of continues in the arcade version, they were like, you're starting back at the beginning.

00:54:17.620 --> 00:54:19.200
I don't care if you give me 50 cents.

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:27.220
So I thought that was funny and just really shows, like, how hard this game can be for certain people.

00:54:27.580 --> 00:54:33.720
And I'm not, you know, I'm not going to say that I didn't beat the, you know, I beat the game with cheats.

00:54:33.720 --> 00:54:36.380
Like, I didn't beat the, I beat the game with the Konami code.

00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:41.840
I did too, yeah. But this isn't a game that rewards, like, other kinds of skills.

00:54:42.020 --> 00:54:43.420
Like, Sean and I were talking about rewinding.

00:54:43.720 --> 00:54:47.560
This game doesn't really reward people who, like, use an emulator and rewind.

00:54:47.980 --> 00:54:50.240
Yeah, I'm not learning any strategy by doing that.

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:57.940
Yeah. I imagine it would also make it harder because sometimes it's, sometimes, well, sometimes it's actually, like, easier sometimes to just die and keep going.

00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:00.460
Like, it just, all right, well, now I know exactly where I die.

00:55:00.540 --> 00:55:03.260
I can just shoot that guy right away because you respawn pretty quickly right away.

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:13.340
The unfortunate thing about this game that I know it is some people's fancy, but I don't really get the same thing.

00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:19.540
I don't get the same amount of pleasure out of a game that it's really just rewarding memorization.

00:55:19.600 --> 00:55:23.800
Because I think that you can get really good at this game if you just memorize the levels.

00:55:24.080 --> 00:55:25.700
And I get it.

00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:29.800
Like, you should probably know when, like, some set piece is coming up.

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:33.760
Or when certain situations may happen.

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:42.440
But I prefer to play games that are more just systems mastery than just memorization of a level.

00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:44.960
Yeah. The whole point is to feel like you're reacting to things.

00:55:44.980 --> 00:55:48.740
Yeah. And not just putting in and planning out what inputs you're going to put in beforehand.

00:55:48.740 --> 00:55:50.260
I still enjoyed playing this game.

00:55:50.380 --> 00:55:53.180
But, like, the concept kind of bothered me.

00:55:53.180 --> 00:55:55.700
I enjoyed playing this game because I didn't memorize it yet.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:55:57.440
So I still felt like it was reactionary.

00:55:57.520 --> 00:56:03.160
I think it does take a special kind of dedication to get to the point where you've got enough of it memorized.

00:56:03.460 --> 00:56:04.580
But people do that.

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:10.400
Yeah. If we're talking about, like, hard but realistic games on the NES that we've played so far,

00:56:10.580 --> 00:56:13.740
I would put this, actually, maybe right with Mega Man.

00:56:14.280 --> 00:56:16.780
Yeah. Maybe it's slightly harder than Mega Man, actually.

00:56:17.020 --> 00:56:19.820
But I think, like, it's right about there.

00:56:19.820 --> 00:56:22.880
Which is a totally fair game in the NES days, in my opinion.

00:56:23.060 --> 00:56:26.260
I don't think this game is being cheap or unfair in any way.

00:56:26.380 --> 00:56:33.480
I did make a note of the only cheap spawn in the game, which happens in the vertical stage in Stage 3,

00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:37.960
where you won't know it unless you land on a specific platform.

00:56:37.960 --> 00:56:42.020
But all of a sudden, a turret will just spawn as soon as you stand on there.

00:56:42.160 --> 00:56:43.760
And it's, like, it's kind of funny.

00:56:43.760 --> 00:56:45.300
So I dismissed it.

00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:48.660
But I was like, you know, I was like, oh, did they really?

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:50.420
Because, you know, they're placing the enemies.

00:56:50.560 --> 00:56:51.580
We always talk about enemy placement.

00:56:51.660 --> 00:56:53.380
They're placing the enemies where they want them.

00:56:53.480 --> 00:56:57.380
And they've somehow solved the Mega Man issue that we had where it was like,

00:56:57.640 --> 00:57:03.560
oh, but if you go backwards a little bit, it'll unspawn and respawn the enemy

00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:05.480
and you'll have to redo all the damage you did to it.

00:57:05.600 --> 00:57:09.660
In this one, they don't let you, like, go backwards at all.

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:13.060
Or even, like, have the chance to re-encounter enemies.

00:57:13.060 --> 00:57:18.520
There are the, like, one-hit-kill henchmen guys that, like, constantly will run into the screen.

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:19.940
Yeah, they come out of a spawn box or something.

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:22.780
But I kind of feel like that's, you know, a necessary evil, too,

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:25.700
because otherwise you might be inclined to just sit still.

00:57:25.880 --> 00:57:29.300
Yeah, for a game this fast-paced, it's got to keep you occupied in some way.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:36.540
Right. Yeah. And we did notice that in our co-op playthroughs and, I think, in our single playthroughs of if you're

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:43.700
standing on, like, the left side of the screen for a while, there will just be a henchman just kind of running.

00:57:43.700 --> 00:57:44.780
Yeah, let's keep going.

00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:48.100
Right, yeah. Running and kind of provoke you a little bit to move.

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:53.020
Or if you're really hugging that side of the screen, you'll just instantly die and be not sure why.

00:57:55.220 --> 00:57:57.360
Okay. What else did I say?

00:57:57.360 --> 00:57:59.460
What did we think of multiplayer?

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:01.220
Oh, yeah, let's talk about co-op.

00:58:01.440 --> 00:58:05.980
First off, this is the first, like, in my opinion, true co-op game.

00:58:06.240 --> 00:58:12.280
Yeah, absolutely. I think it was sold at the time with that in mind, that co-op would be the focus.

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:17.160
They put the two main players on the front cover for a reason.

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:18.720
They want you to have it.

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:23.660
Well, we're forgetting the flying game where you fly to the left.

00:58:24.520 --> 00:58:25.600
Oh, Sky Kid?

00:58:25.600 --> 00:58:28.320
Yeah, that's got, like, two characters for it.

00:58:28.440 --> 00:58:29.260
It does. The birds.

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:30.540
Yeah, the bird planes.

00:58:30.680 --> 00:58:33.900
That you wouldn't know. You would just think you're controlling air propeller planes, but whatever.

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:37.600
Yeah, no, I guess I mean true co-op in the sense of, like...

00:58:38.180 --> 00:58:40.680
Fun games. Yeah, and you're experiencing...

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:45.240
But you're experiencing it together, and you're going through an actual journey.

00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:49.300
Sky Kid is more like you're just kind of, like, shooting through waves of enemies and

00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:50.420
it's like, on to the next stage.

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:52.680
This is a game where you can say, like, get that guy.

00:58:52.780 --> 00:58:53.680
I got that guy over here.

00:58:53.760 --> 00:58:58.020
You can plan things and you can, like, communicate like you're actually on a battlefield together.

00:58:58.120 --> 00:58:59.520
I don't know. I've never been on one, but...

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:00.800
So truly.

00:59:01.220 --> 00:59:02.600
Like, that is the experience.

00:59:02.820 --> 00:59:04.120
We got a patriot over here.

00:59:05.400 --> 00:59:10.260
And then also, Joe, I'm glad you made that analogy, though, too, because this is...

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:15.020
There are only a few games so far that we've played together that, like, have caused that

00:59:15.020 --> 00:59:16.160
kind of, like, talk.

00:59:16.360 --> 00:59:19.160
But you're right. To Sean's point, the game is fast-paced.

00:59:19.280 --> 00:59:22.340
And to your point, that brings about conversations where you're talking really fast.

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:26.020
I think it's just a really exciting experience when you add in a second person.

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:31.760
It is exciting, but we can see in the way that this game was...

00:59:31.760 --> 00:59:35.220
That the multiplayer was actually implemented that it's not perfect yet.

00:59:35.480 --> 00:59:37.160
It feels like the multiplayer...

00:59:37.160 --> 00:59:41.880
It's weird to say that the game was sold on a co-op basis because the way that it's designed

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:45.840
in co-op, it definitely feels like an afterthought of, like, well, you know, there is a second

00:59:45.840 --> 00:59:49.140
controller on this system and we could emulate a second player.

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:55.040
Because the weird thing about co-op in this one is that I'm used to a game where whoever's,

00:59:55.040 --> 00:59:59.580
like, moving forward controls the screen and pushes it along.

00:59:59.580 --> 01:00:04.000
And the other person who's, like, lagging behind just gets, like, the scroll just kind

01:00:04.000 --> 01:00:05.760
of, like, continues to drag them along.

01:00:05.880 --> 01:00:09.940
Well, that's like a Sonic and Tails sort of thing where Tails was just, like, at the whim

01:00:09.940 --> 01:00:10.960
of Sonic. Right.

01:00:11.160 --> 01:00:18.940
I mean, I think that most games with this sort of multiplayer aspect will wait for you,

01:00:19.140 --> 01:00:22.760
but will wait for both players to sort of, like, go ahead.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:30.880
Um, but I think that the constraints that the screen has on you in this game specifically

01:00:30.880 --> 01:00:32.880
are a lot more fickle.

01:00:33.400 --> 01:00:39.040
Um, you have to, like, go past a certain point on the screen to get it to budge just a little

01:00:39.040 --> 01:00:44.060
bit and that can affect your ability to finish jumps.

01:00:44.060 --> 01:00:48.600
Yeah, it feels like you can maybe trap yourself a little bit, which I think was a problem.

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:53.040
The only reason it didn't, other than the trapping yourself, it didn't bother me quite as much

01:00:53.040 --> 01:00:58.900
is after playing it single player and then playing it with you guys, there were a few

01:00:58.900 --> 01:01:02.480
things that were tough for me single player that were, like, kind of really easy for us

01:01:02.480 --> 01:01:03.660
with two players.

01:01:03.840 --> 01:01:04.780
Like, those tanks.

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:06.320
Yeah. We took those out, no problem.

01:01:06.320 --> 01:01:10.900
Some of the bosses. They didn't double up the health, which I think is an inappropriate move.

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:16.140
Right, but I did think it was, it added another level of challenge, even if it wasn't intended

01:01:16.140 --> 01:01:20.400
and it might be a little funky, it was kind of interesting a few times where, like, one

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:24.960
of us would be, would have died and the other one has to wait for them to come back to, like,

01:01:25.040 --> 01:01:26.040
all right, get over this jump.

01:01:26.140 --> 01:01:29.620
Like, I'm covering, or someone's covering the other person while I try and make the jump

01:01:29.620 --> 01:01:32.200
so you can both move forward rather than just moving forward and dragging.

01:01:32.460 --> 01:01:37.200
There's a coordination element to the co-op where you both have to be on the same page of

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:40.860
when you're even going to progress because, like we said, the camera,

01:01:40.900 --> 01:01:44.980
kind of locks in, in like this middle portion of the stage.

01:01:45.160 --> 01:01:50.000
So if you're both not, like, ready to move on, the camera doesn't, like, allow the player

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:52.700
who's in the lead to keep pushing the other player along.

01:01:52.860 --> 01:01:56.580
Yeah. On the topic of coordination, though, and where I think that you guys are giving a little

01:01:56.580 --> 01:02:03.040
too much credit to the multiplayer, is that I am all for using coordination for a two-player

01:02:03.040 --> 01:02:06.240
game. Uh, that's how these things should be played.

01:02:06.360 --> 01:02:13.960
But I think that coordinating to defeat an enemy, uh, with strategy is different than

01:02:13.960 --> 01:02:20.600
coordinating to, like, fight against the game itself from, like, keeping you in these weird,

01:02:21.040 --> 01:02:21.960
like, awkward positions.

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:23.440
I think that's very different.

01:02:23.440 --> 01:02:25.900
Like, you're trying to fight the game, not the enemies in the game.

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:33.440
Oh, absolutely. And I would even say that, uh, it goes against the, the game's feel because it's slowing down

01:02:33.440 --> 01:02:39.400
the game considerably when all of a sudden you have to, like, help player two, like, get

01:02:39.400 --> 01:02:40.300
across the screen.

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:41.880
Like, come on, we gotta keep moving.

01:02:41.880 --> 01:02:46.700
And then the enemies spawn, like, the first pixel that's possible for them.

01:02:46.800 --> 01:02:51.700
Yeah. And then you have to, like, be prepared to, like, duck because you're not sure if they're

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:52.760
gonna shoot or not right away.

01:02:52.900 --> 01:02:59.140
Right. Yeah. I definitely, you know, will say that the, that positive aspect I was talking about is

01:02:59.140 --> 01:03:03.100
definitely me seeing a positive in what's clearly, like, a, like a flaw in the design.

01:03:03.360 --> 01:03:06.040
Yeah. True. And it's just, it's, you know, definitely wasn't intended that way.

01:03:06.040 --> 01:03:11.320
I just thought that on the, on the, in the cases that it wasn't, like, broken, it was

01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:15.460
kind of, it was kind of fun to, like, to have to try and help your teammate get to the other

01:03:15.460 --> 01:03:16.260
side so you can progress.

01:03:16.460 --> 01:03:20.820
I'd argue that the co-op is neat enough that it's in there and I'm glad that it's included,

01:03:21.040 --> 01:03:23.720
but that the single player actually feels like a better experience.

01:03:23.840 --> 01:03:29.580
Yes. I, I, cause I think objectively I agree with that, but, like, personally, I just always

01:03:29.580 --> 01:03:32.460
like a co-op game, especially an NES game.

01:03:32.540 --> 01:03:35.600
It's just, it's just so interesting to me to be able to, like, work with someone else on

01:03:35.600 --> 01:03:40.480
that. You know, not. But yeah, objectively I think it's a better, a more polished game single player.

01:03:40.480 --> 01:03:45.300
Sure. It kind of reminds me almost of, like, new Super Mario Brothers Wii when that came out.

01:03:45.460 --> 01:03:46.260
It's like chaos.

01:03:46.600 --> 01:03:49.240
Yeah, cause they finally allowed, like, four players.

01:03:49.380 --> 01:03:54.520
One person would be Mario, one Luigi, two Toads, and it was, like, player one who's, like,

01:03:54.620 --> 01:03:57.100
probably the, you know, the gamer of the family.

01:03:57.360 --> 01:04:00.980
The gamer. Yeah, you know, as good as, as good as he wants, but no, no, now he's, like, you know,

01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:03.400
his three, like, his mom might be playing with him.

01:04:03.440 --> 01:04:05.380
This is, like, Christmas Day, they just got the game, you know.

01:04:05.380 --> 01:04:07.700
It's like, give us the back story to this.

01:04:10.040 --> 01:04:11.140
Is his name Mike?

01:04:11.280 --> 01:04:15.900
Babe. You've got three, you've got three other people who might not be as good as the first

01:04:15.900 --> 01:04:19.160
player, and you don't get rewarded for being the good player.

01:04:19.160 --> 01:04:23.820
Everybody else just kind of has to, like, get with the program and keep moving, you know?

01:04:23.980 --> 01:04:25.720
And it's like, that's what it reminds me of.

01:04:25.800 --> 01:04:31.800
And it wasn't good there in New Super Mario Brothers Wii, and it's not good here for those

01:04:31.800 --> 01:04:33.960
same reasons. But I get what you're saying.

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:39.680
That almost, like, that added chaos on a, on a social level is kind of fun.

01:04:39.780 --> 01:04:42.640
Like, like, the way that you talk to each other because of those things.

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:44.980
You were even being funny, Sean, with the co-op.

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:45.960
You were like... Even you.

01:04:46.180 --> 01:04:47.880
Yeah. Even Sean was funny.

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:49.780
I'm never funny. No, but even just, like, watching.

01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:53.860
Like, when we were, when me and Joe were playing, your commentary is almost playing.

01:04:54.200 --> 01:04:59.380
It's like, you know, it makes a, what should just be a straight single player game into

01:04:59.380 --> 01:05:00.480
a more, like... No talking.

01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:01.640
Bring the friends over.

01:05:01.780 --> 01:05:03.360
Right, exactly. Bring the friends over.

01:05:03.480 --> 01:05:04.540
Everybody takes a turn.

01:05:04.700 --> 01:05:05.860
Everybody gets in on it.

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:07.640
And everybody can talk about what's happening.

01:05:07.640 --> 01:05:10.260
Yeah, multiplayer is always fun for the most part.

01:05:10.580 --> 01:05:14.760
Right. But we haven't seen many, like, awesome multiplayer games on the NES yet, I feel.

01:05:14.900 --> 01:05:18.920
That's true. Yeah. You guys all missed us when we were playing baseball together.

01:05:20.200 --> 01:05:23.940
Should there have been an inventory in Contra?

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:25.600
No. Good.

01:05:26.380 --> 01:05:30.620
Joe? I mean, I guess I don't think it was necessarily a negative thing, but I think it was fine without.

01:05:30.800 --> 01:05:33.120
I don't think it needed... I think it would make it a totally different game.

01:05:33.620 --> 01:05:36.260
So, like, I'm open to hear an argument to it, but...

01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:42.040
More like, think of it as you can store a second power-up as, like, a...

01:05:42.040 --> 01:05:48.500
I'm going to switch between my spread and my machine gun, so I can hit select.

01:05:48.860 --> 01:05:54.140
Maybe if you're storing one other power-up and you still lose them both if you die, then

01:05:54.140 --> 01:05:55.960
I think it keeps the experience attack.

01:05:56.080 --> 01:05:59.680
But if you have, like, an inventory of power-ups, then I think it's...

01:05:59.680 --> 01:06:03.740
You get them all too soon, and it would make it a different, like, type of game.

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:06.560
I don't know. Yeah, I think that this is...

01:06:07.060 --> 01:06:10.340
That it would be a detriment for this to have an inventory system.

01:06:10.540 --> 01:06:16.520
And even the stock one thing, I think that this is a game that you're just supposed to

01:06:16.520 --> 01:06:23.340
get into flow. You're not supposed to be thinking about where you may have a, like, oh, I can switch to this

01:06:23.340 --> 01:06:26.660
now. Like, no, you just have what you have and go through it.

01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:28.380
Yeah, I completely agree.

01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:34.060
I bring it up because there is a ROM hack available on ROMhacking.net where you can...

01:06:34.060 --> 01:06:35.960
They actually, like, put a box around it and everything.

01:06:36.060 --> 01:06:36.980
You can select...

01:06:36.980 --> 01:06:39.980
You can hit the select button to change between the two ammunitions.

01:06:40.160 --> 01:06:41.140
I feel like...

01:06:41.140 --> 01:06:43.260
Are there potions? There's no potions or anything like that.

01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:45.780
There's no inventory... Sorry, no inventory screen like that.

01:06:45.780 --> 01:06:50.100
It's just, like, you can switch between whichever your most recent and your second most recent.

01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:50.980
Spread and fireball.

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:53.200
Yeah, exactly. You can switch between those.

01:06:53.200 --> 01:06:58.340
Whenever you acquire a new power-up, the one that was second in place is now gone.

01:06:58.440 --> 01:06:59.600
Yeah. And you pick up this new one.

01:06:59.780 --> 01:07:01.180
I just... I agree with you.

01:07:01.340 --> 01:07:07.500
I think, like, the power-ups don't change the game on a fundamental level that you would

01:07:07.500 --> 01:07:11.940
need to, like, be like, I need to hold on to this specific one or I can't...

01:07:11.940 --> 01:07:13.000
You know, it's like...

01:07:13.000 --> 01:07:15.440
I agree that this is more like a...

01:07:15.440 --> 01:07:17.740
If you don't want that power-up, you just don't pick it up.

01:07:18.420 --> 01:07:25.740
Exactly. We talked about the Famicom version having, like, the additional forestry where the trees

01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:28.560
would actually sway and, like, the grass would move.

01:07:28.720 --> 01:07:32.560
But there's more to the Famicom version than that.

01:07:32.800 --> 01:07:35.380
The Famicom version actually contains cutscenes.

01:07:36.260 --> 01:07:37.200
See, I think it could have helped.

01:07:37.300 --> 01:07:38.780
It could have had cutscenes.

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:39.980
I think the cutscenes would have been awesome.

01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:43.780
And if you check them out, I'll put it in the show notes, a link to the long play of

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:45.920
the Famicom version. The cutscenes are awesome.

01:07:45.920 --> 01:07:49.760
But there's also, like, a map, which is kind of cool, of, like, that overworld, like,

01:07:50.100 --> 01:07:52.460
you're in Section A right now.

01:07:52.480 --> 01:07:53.340
A Castlevania map.

01:07:53.480 --> 01:07:54.760
Yeah, a Castlevania-style map.

01:07:56.240 --> 01:07:57.140
There's also... What else?

01:07:57.180 --> 01:07:58.600
There's an additional music track.

01:07:58.820 --> 01:07:59.800
Music in this game was fire.

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:00.780
We didn't really talk about that.

01:08:00.880 --> 01:08:01.740
I thought the music was great.

01:08:01.740 --> 01:08:05.300
It was great, yeah. Konami, man, they're killing it on the NES for us.

01:08:05.320 --> 01:08:06.300
It makes me so sad.

01:08:06.600 --> 01:08:08.220
I know, right? What they are today.

01:08:08.500 --> 01:08:14.920
Mm-hmm. So all that stuff was added because the Famicom version, at that point, they were able to,

01:08:14.920 --> 01:08:17.140
like, add additional chips and stuff like that.

01:08:17.420 --> 01:08:23.080
They weren't going to do that for the NES version, so we get the lackluster version.

01:08:23.200 --> 01:08:25.220
But I guess, like, you know, growing up, no one knew.

01:08:25.580 --> 01:08:26.560
Yeah. And I didn't...

01:08:26.560 --> 01:08:27.780
I played only the NES version.

01:08:27.880 --> 01:08:30.460
I didn't play through, like, a translated Famicom cart.

01:08:30.860 --> 01:08:32.580
So I thought, like, this was fine.

01:08:32.580 --> 01:08:36.720
It's definitely, like, the Famicom version, from what I've seen of it, is the superior game,

01:08:36.760 --> 01:08:40.740
but that doesn't diminish how good the NES game is.

01:08:41.020 --> 01:08:48.300
And there's also a hidden message in the Famicom Contra version, which is really cool.

01:08:48.420 --> 01:08:56.600
To do it, you have to beat the game, hold down start and select, and then from the moment you beat the game.

01:08:56.760 --> 01:09:01.520
And then after the credits completely roll through, I'm talking, like, every single name,

01:09:01.520 --> 01:09:03.620
the Konami logo flashes on the screen.

01:09:04.160 --> 01:09:09.120
All of a sudden, there's a seizure-inducing, like, blue and red series of flashes.

01:09:09.740 --> 01:09:11.600
And then it types...

01:09:11.600 --> 01:09:12.540
Kill your parents.

01:09:13.140 --> 01:09:14.060
Okay, whoa.

01:09:15.140 --> 01:09:16.340
One letter at a time.

01:09:16.360 --> 01:09:17.980
Yeah, remarkably slowly.

01:09:18.300 --> 01:09:19.980
Yeah, it's like...

01:09:19.980 --> 01:09:26.180
Yeah. As I was saying, it's almost like the alien is, like, doesn't know how to type on a keyboard.

01:09:26.300 --> 01:09:29.540
It's, like, using one finger, like, when you first learn how to, like, use a keyboard.

01:09:29.700 --> 01:09:34.780
Use the keyboard. We didn't really see aliens have fingers, right?

01:09:35.020 --> 01:09:36.000
That's true, yeah. Like, we didn't...

01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:37.120
Any of the aliens in the game...

01:09:37.120 --> 01:09:38.460
I mean, I think the...

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:43.780
If we're going to go back to the enemies real quick, there was a facehugger alien.

01:09:43.940 --> 01:09:50.700
Yeah. Then there was the little xenomorph alien that sort of spun around in the air.

01:09:50.920 --> 01:09:52.280
Yeah, they float. Yeah.

01:09:52.460 --> 01:09:55.300
And then there was the giant xenomorph that you had to shoot.

01:09:55.380 --> 01:09:56.880
Yeah, I think it's the giant xenomorph.

01:09:57.160 --> 01:10:00.000
Yeah, he's the one. Like, his neck and his head, the type that's messed up.

01:10:00.020 --> 01:10:02.160
And then there was the heart. All right.

01:10:02.340 --> 01:10:05.140
Yes. Well, the heart is because you're inside the alien.

01:10:05.260 --> 01:10:09.240
It's true. I'm going to keep everybody on suspense for what the hidden message contains for a minute,

01:10:09.280 --> 01:10:11.040
because we should talk about that.

01:10:11.280 --> 01:10:14.720
Yeah. Like, the xenomorph head is, like...

01:10:14.720 --> 01:10:18.900
I feel like that's almost, like, iconic imagery in the NES, you know, to see something...

01:10:18.900 --> 01:10:20.520
Like, that's beautiful sprite work.

01:10:20.720 --> 01:10:22.220
Yeah, yeah. And to see that...

01:10:22.220 --> 01:10:25.800
And to have that not be the final boss, like, it was like a psych-out, right?

01:10:25.860 --> 01:10:29.500
Yeah. I was thinking when I was playing, I was like, why is this final boss, like, easier than

01:10:29.500 --> 01:10:30.440
all the other bosses?

01:10:30.660 --> 01:10:31.960
Turns out that's not the final boss.

01:10:31.960 --> 01:10:37.200
But even the final boss, when you see it, first off, it reminded me very much of Mother

01:10:37.200 --> 01:10:38.060
Brain. Yeah.

01:10:38.500 --> 01:10:39.480
Because it's...

01:10:39.480 --> 01:10:40.660
I said that, actually.

01:10:40.740 --> 01:10:43.000
Because I was like, wait, is this Mother Brain?

01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:44.140
I forgot which one was Mother Brain.

01:10:44.240 --> 01:10:46.400
It wasn't, but I said that when I was playing it, but...

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:52.620
Yeah, it's just like the, I guess, like the main organ of whatever the alien layer is made

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:54.600
out of. And then it...

01:10:54.600 --> 01:10:59.400
The actual, like, difficulty of the boss fight comes from this creature, this enemy that

01:10:59.400 --> 01:11:00.760
you've just been introduced to.

01:11:00.860 --> 01:11:02.560
Yeah. It's not alluded to anywhere else in the game.

01:11:02.560 --> 01:11:03.460
Basically the facehugger thing.

01:11:03.780 --> 01:11:09.600
Yeah. They move very slowly, and you take care of them, and then if you don't take care of them,

01:11:09.680 --> 01:11:11.060
they spawn into those...

01:11:11.060 --> 01:11:12.300
Oh, you see what you're talking about.

01:11:12.380 --> 01:11:14.140
Yeah. At first, they're like these white puff balls.

01:11:14.140 --> 01:11:18.120
Yeah. That's a different enemy than what I was referring to.

01:11:18.200 --> 01:11:25.180
Oh, okay. What I was referring to were the enemies that would spawn from the floor or the ceiling surrounding

01:11:25.180 --> 01:11:27.320
the heart that would ever crawl.

01:11:27.320 --> 01:11:28.360
Oh, the tentacle creatures.

01:11:28.440 --> 01:11:33.260
Yes. So that's what I likened to the facehugger from Alien.

01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:38.260
Got it. Whereas, yeah, those enemies I hate and I never want to see again in any game because

01:11:38.260 --> 01:11:41.560
they were just like, they would just home in on you.

01:11:41.720 --> 01:11:42.800
And it was very hard.

01:11:42.940 --> 01:11:49.180
I don't like it. I'd say overall, like, the bosses of the game were tough.

01:11:49.700 --> 01:11:51.740
Some were even tougher than others.

01:11:51.880 --> 01:12:00.980
I felt like the stage four boss with the heads and the brains that shoot the bubbles and target

01:12:00.980 --> 01:12:02.120
the homing bubbles.

01:12:02.120 --> 01:12:06.180
Yeah. I thought that was a very challenging boss that kind of, like, all of a sudden told

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:08.740
me, like, this game isn't fun anymore right now.

01:12:08.900 --> 01:12:11.400
You know? Like, you have to figure out what to do.

01:12:12.560 --> 01:12:14.220
You know, there's moments like that.

01:12:14.220 --> 01:12:19.020
But I think all of the bosses definitely deserve points from us for how creative and different

01:12:19.020 --> 01:12:19.900
they are from each other.

01:12:20.460 --> 01:12:21.900
Really the whole game, to be honest.

01:12:22.080 --> 01:12:24.320
Even each level feels like its own.

01:12:24.500 --> 01:12:26.280
There's a great sense of variety.

01:12:26.740 --> 01:12:30.060
And I think I said early on in this episode, I said seven.

01:12:30.200 --> 01:12:31.440
There's eight stages total.

01:12:32.020 --> 01:12:37.120
And I think each of the eight stages is just long enough that it doesn't overstay its welcome

01:12:37.120 --> 01:12:38.360
at any particular point.

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:40.640
I think it's really well polished in that regard.

01:12:40.780 --> 01:12:46.580
And not to derail us, but we didn't really mention how, like, each stage has its own, like,

01:12:46.700 --> 01:12:47.620
thing you're doing.

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:51.160
Like, you know, the first stage is just you're getting ready, you're getting warmed up.

01:12:51.240 --> 01:12:52.080
Each side-scrolling stage.

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:53.880
The first stage, you're getting warmed up, you know how to play the game.

01:12:54.060 --> 01:12:57.300
Second stage, you're jumping and going up the waterfall.

01:12:58.080 --> 01:13:00.400
Third stage is the snow stage, correct?

01:13:00.700 --> 01:13:03.600
Yeah. And that's when there's the bombs coming from the environment that you can attack.

01:13:04.220 --> 01:13:06.000
And probably also for the other stages.

01:13:06.260 --> 01:13:07.560
Those are the three that I remember.

01:13:07.860 --> 01:13:10.580
No, yeah. Later stages also just feel unique in the way they're designed.

01:13:10.780 --> 01:13:11.700
They have new hazards.

01:13:12.140 --> 01:13:16.080
Exactly, yeah. Like the carts and the walls of spikes and the fire.

01:13:16.580 --> 01:13:20.660
And they're also, you know, from a motif standpoint, too, they're...

01:13:21.420 --> 01:13:23.300
Everything is more caved in, you know?

01:13:23.360 --> 01:13:24.280
It's claustrophobic.

01:13:24.520 --> 01:13:28.320
You don't have the freedom to just kind of jump around wherever you want.

01:13:28.880 --> 01:13:30.980
And then the alien lair is just awesome.

01:13:31.240 --> 01:13:34.820
Yeah, it is cool. It feels like you're inside of a giant alien.

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:37.120
Yeah. So I really like all that.

01:13:37.280 --> 01:13:41.100
And then when you... If you do beat the game, the Amazon just blows up.

01:13:41.400 --> 01:13:43.220
Yeah. The island of the Amazon.

01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:45.160
Right. It just sounds terrible.

01:13:45.760 --> 01:13:48.120
It's like, okay. It's like a tiny little island.

01:13:48.160 --> 01:13:49.940
I think Brazil is doing that anyway right now.

01:13:50.020 --> 01:13:51.040
So it's like, whatever.

01:13:51.160 --> 01:13:52.300
They're not taking donations.

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:58.720
They're going to fix it. So the hidden message, because everybody's dying to know what it is, it's good that we

01:13:58.720 --> 01:14:02.640
brought this back full circle, because if you beat the game, you will know that you have

01:14:02.640 --> 01:14:06.540
defeated the aliens, the xenomorphs, the facehuggers, whatever you have.

01:14:06.740 --> 01:14:14.360
And then this thing will slowly type onto the screen in red text, foolish earthlings, did

01:14:14.360 --> 01:14:17.320
you really think you destroyed us?

01:14:17.320 --> 01:14:24.880
We will take over all of Earth's spaces, and by the time you realize it, it will be too

01:14:24.880 --> 01:14:29.600
late. Red Falcon will live forever.

01:14:34.520 --> 01:14:36.280
Man. Five dots.

01:14:36.720 --> 01:14:40.700
Wow. Joe, you had a theory about why they were taking so long to type.

01:14:40.900 --> 01:14:42.000
Yeah. I thought it was going to...

01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:44.880
While it was typing, I thought it was going to end with...

01:14:45.740 --> 01:14:50.420
And while you were distracted reading this message, we've been taking over Earth.

01:14:51.400 --> 01:14:52.480
They took that long.

01:14:52.500 --> 01:14:54.400
And that's why they were going so slow.

01:14:54.680 --> 01:14:55.960
Now that's pretty cool, though, right?

01:14:56.000 --> 01:15:00.300
A hidden message. Like, there's no way anybody thought, like, oh, I'll just hold down start and select for

01:15:00.300 --> 01:15:01.640
the entirety of the credits.

01:15:02.340 --> 01:15:08.380
So I thought that was really cool, and it's actually, it's teasing the next Contra game.

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:10.460
So we'll get into that in a second.

01:15:10.460 --> 01:15:14.460
And the only other thing I want to talk about with the Famicom version is that I found a

01:15:15.080 --> 01:15:18.020
commercial for Contra in Japan.

01:15:18.960 --> 01:15:20.780
And I'll just link it to the show notes.

01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:22.280
We're not going to play the audio or anything.

01:15:22.700 --> 01:15:29.900
But it's specifically weird because it calls out, like, three ways of scrolling or something

01:15:29.900 --> 01:15:31.120
like that, right? Isn't that...

01:15:31.120 --> 01:15:32.700
Yeah, they call it three-way scrolling, right?

01:15:32.740 --> 01:15:37.860
Three-way scrolling. They call it something as if it's a, like, tri-directional scrolling or something.

01:15:37.860 --> 01:15:41.440
They're talking about, like, the features of the game, you know, like directional attacks

01:15:41.440 --> 01:15:42.980
and three-way scrolling.

01:15:43.140 --> 01:15:44.060
And I'm like, what does that mean?

01:15:44.300 --> 01:15:46.480
And it means... We were, like, breaking it down a little bit.

01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:47.800
Right. You scroll to the right.

01:15:47.960 --> 01:15:48.860
Yeah, but not to the left.

01:15:49.040 --> 01:15:50.500
Right. Not to the left. You can go up.

01:15:50.520 --> 01:15:51.380
You scroll up.

01:15:51.580 --> 01:15:52.620
Not down. In.

01:15:52.620 --> 01:15:54.020
And in, but not out.

01:15:54.400 --> 01:15:55.560
This is the first NES game.

01:15:55.560 --> 01:15:58.520
It's just funny that the three ways are not, like, opposites of each other.

01:15:58.760 --> 01:16:02.780
Right. The first NES game to feature three-way scrolling.

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:04.240
Make note of it.

01:16:04.600 --> 01:16:08.920
And then it just has, like, over this, you know, it's English dialogue on this Japanese

01:16:08.920 --> 01:16:14.680
commercial before it starts getting into, like, the end thing about payment and MSRP and

01:16:14.680 --> 01:16:22.000
stuff like that. Otherwise, it's very creepy music and, like, weird alien imagery.

01:16:22.520 --> 01:16:24.460
So you're not really...

01:16:24.460 --> 01:16:25.760
You don't see any gameplay.

01:16:26.020 --> 01:16:28.720
It doesn't show you, like, what Contra looks like on the NES.

01:16:28.980 --> 01:16:34.780
It just shows you these alien creatures and describes, like, you know, you have eight

01:16:34.780 --> 01:16:35.980
directions of control.

01:16:36.300 --> 01:16:37.900
Like, great.

01:16:38.260 --> 01:16:42.540
It said, like, it uses an action chip or something, too, or whatever.

01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:53.160
Anyway, I think it's time we talk about sequels and spinoffs.

01:16:53.460 --> 01:16:54.960
Sean, would you like to walk us through this?

01:16:55.800 --> 01:17:00.600
Uh, wait, so is there, like, is there going to be another one on the NES?

01:17:00.880 --> 01:17:04.100
Yeah. Well, what do you know about the Contra franchise in general?

01:17:04.300 --> 01:17:05.400
Like, anything?

01:17:05.740 --> 01:17:11.400
Well, I know that the more recent ones are, like, you know, they got all the cool sprite

01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:18.200
work and they're fun and hard and fast and I like turtles.

01:17:18.200 --> 01:17:19.280
And sometimes rare.

01:17:19.720 --> 01:17:22.240
Uh, they're worth a lot of money, some of these Contra games.

01:17:22.620 --> 01:17:24.000
But, uh, I'll help you out.

01:17:24.140 --> 01:17:25.100
Okay? Thanks, Mike.

01:17:25.780 --> 01:17:29.720
Super C is the game of the next Contra game.

01:17:29.840 --> 01:17:33.320
Obviously, you guys would know that Super C, C stands for Contra, right?

01:17:33.460 --> 01:17:35.620
It just seems like, are they trying to high-sepray that?

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:37.360
Just call it Super Contra.

01:17:37.940 --> 01:17:39.560
No, it comes to the NES.

01:17:40.000 --> 01:17:40.940
So we will play it.

01:17:41.020 --> 01:17:44.040
In 1990. So look out for that for it.

01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:46.920
Episode 400 or 500, who knows?

01:17:47.340 --> 01:17:54.640
Look out for that. This time, the alien forces have taken over an allied military base possessing most of its

01:17:54.640 --> 01:17:56.520
troops. Sounds like the hidden message.

01:17:56.720 --> 01:18:00.080
They said they were going to come after the bases and now they've actually taken over.

01:18:00.200 --> 01:18:01.300
Did they say base? Yeah.

01:18:01.420 --> 01:18:03.200
They did. They said, uh.

01:18:03.200 --> 01:18:04.600
None of your bases are safe or something.

01:18:04.720 --> 01:18:06.940
Right. They'll take over all of Earth's bases.

01:18:07.320 --> 01:18:08.300
So there you go.

01:18:08.360 --> 01:18:09.660
It's a little tease to Super C.

01:18:10.120 --> 01:18:15.360
Nice. And then in 1992, Contra Force comes to the NES as well.

01:18:15.420 --> 01:18:16.920
So a third Contra game.

01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:19.620
We're going to be playing these games in 1992?

01:18:20.740 --> 01:18:23.220
Yeah. The NES ends in 1994.

01:18:23.760 --> 01:18:25.200
Oh, dear. So there you go.

01:18:26.300 --> 01:18:27.160
The villains.

01:18:27.600 --> 01:18:32.600
Oh, sorry. So in Contra Force, the description of the game is that the villains in this game are

01:18:32.600 --> 01:18:35.540
human terrorists and there's no mention of aliens.

01:18:36.460 --> 01:18:37.340
That's interesting.

01:18:37.880 --> 01:18:39.760
I don't like that. I think those are the aliens.

01:18:39.760 --> 01:18:41.120
And that's Contra Force.

01:18:42.340 --> 01:18:47.000
It's like, that's, like, I guess Red Falcon is still, like, a terrorist organization.

01:18:47.420 --> 01:18:51.000
But it seems like the stakes are less high now.

01:18:51.100 --> 01:18:52.620
It's like, well, we've fought people before.

01:18:52.820 --> 01:18:53.880
Like, people fight people all the time.

01:18:53.960 --> 01:18:55.580
People fighting aliens. That's, like, a big thing.

01:18:55.980 --> 01:18:57.160
Next, we'll be fighting, like, bugs.

01:18:57.800 --> 01:18:58.860
Man versus man.

01:18:59.200 --> 01:19:00.220
Man versus nature.

01:19:00.780 --> 01:19:01.780
Man versus self.

01:19:02.280 --> 01:19:03.400
Man versus aliens. Right.

01:19:03.500 --> 01:19:08.340
There you go. And it would be another 30, 35 minutes if I went through all the other sequels and spinoffs.

01:19:08.420 --> 01:19:09.360
So I'm not going to do that. So here we go.

01:19:09.660 --> 01:19:11.960
But, yeah. So there, you know, they come to Super Nintendo.

01:19:11.960 --> 01:19:13.260
They eventually go to the Genesis.

01:19:13.500 --> 01:19:14.920
There's a PlayStation 1 game.

01:19:15.080 --> 01:19:16.400
There's, like, a little bit of a hiatus.

01:19:17.220 --> 01:19:23.200
But then there's Contra Road Corps, which came out on September 24th, 2019.

01:19:23.660 --> 01:19:29.180
Wow. What? Yeah, for Nintendo Switch and PS4 and everything, it's out there.

01:19:29.320 --> 01:19:30.660
How have I not seen this yet?

01:19:30.880 --> 01:19:32.020
And it's bad.

01:19:33.060 --> 01:19:34.500
People do not like it.

01:19:34.740 --> 01:19:40.520
It looks nothing like Contra, the NES game, in the sense of, like, even in style or play.

01:19:41.200 --> 01:19:42.740
It's not a 2D side-scroller.

01:19:43.020 --> 01:19:47.080
It's, like, it does focus a lot on the co-op part of it.

01:19:47.080 --> 01:19:55.380
But imagine, like, four minifigures with, like, a very zoomed-out screen just running around shooting bullets in any direction.

01:19:55.380 --> 01:19:56.760
So it's, like, a twin-stick shooter now?

01:19:57.520 --> 01:20:00.660
Yes. You can think of it like a twin-stick shooter in that regard.

01:20:00.660 --> 01:20:03.200
I think that's the best way to compare it.

01:20:03.280 --> 01:20:04.920
But not, like, top-down.

01:20:05.100 --> 01:20:08.980
It's, like, from a 90-degree view.

01:20:09.280 --> 01:20:10.960
A 90-degree view.

01:20:11.160 --> 01:20:12.320
Yeah, like...

01:20:12.320 --> 01:20:13.900
Top-down? 45-degree angle.

01:20:14.000 --> 01:20:15.620
Oh, okay. So, like an isometric thing.

01:20:15.620 --> 01:20:16.760
Yeah, yeah. Like an isometric.

01:20:16.760 --> 01:20:17.720
There you go. Yeah.

01:20:17.860 --> 01:20:18.940
Very strange-looking.

01:20:19.100 --> 01:20:19.960
I don't know.

01:20:20.060 --> 01:20:21.120
It didn't get great reviews.

01:20:21.340 --> 01:20:26.680
But the story is set several years after Contra 3 The Alien Wars on Super Nintendo.

01:20:27.160 --> 01:20:31.100
And the last thing I do want to mention, since we're in the fun area of the sequels and spinoffs,

01:20:31.180 --> 01:20:36.720
is that the Contra game book is a choose-your-own-adventure-style game book released by Konami

01:20:36.720 --> 01:20:40.280
to go along with the release of their mascot game, Contra.

01:20:40.960 --> 01:20:43.320
Huh. So is it, like, a shoot-up, shoot-right?

01:20:43.520 --> 01:20:45.640
Right. I'm trying to find...

01:20:45.640 --> 01:20:50.120
I so desperately, I want you guys to know I tried to find a copy so that we could play

01:20:50.120 --> 01:20:54.220
the choose-your-own-adventure in the post-show, but I cannot find it.

01:20:54.400 --> 01:20:55.760
Damn. So we won't be doing that.

01:20:56.120 --> 01:20:59.720
If you have a copy and you just want to, like, transcribe it...

01:20:59.720 --> 01:21:01.880
Oh. The whole internet would probably like that.

01:21:02.040 --> 01:21:04.500
So whoever has a copy, you should definitely go ahead and do that.

01:21:05.420 --> 01:21:09.400
However, it is now time for the Essential Games List.

01:21:12.020 --> 01:21:13.680
Sam? Oh, sorry.

01:21:13.920 --> 01:21:15.060
Sorry, guys. I was asleep.

01:21:15.400 --> 01:21:16.280
Yeah, I've been asleep.

01:21:16.600 --> 01:21:21.760
Okay. So just give us your vote, you know, and you can give us a little story since you've...

01:21:22.880 --> 01:21:24.160
We're doing Essentials. Yeah, Essential Games List.

01:21:24.280 --> 01:21:25.220
Yeah. Oh, okay, cool.

01:21:25.600 --> 01:21:27.380
So what do you got? Contra.

01:21:27.560 --> 01:21:28.760
Oh, right, right, right.

01:21:31.820 --> 01:21:32.680
It's a good game.

01:21:34.140 --> 01:21:38.620
It's, you know, I'm sure we're going to see more games that are like this, but this is,

01:21:38.620 --> 01:21:40.200
like, the game, you know?

01:21:40.420 --> 01:21:41.280
This is the...

01:21:41.280 --> 01:21:42.400
The only video game.

01:21:42.460 --> 01:21:43.260
The run and gun.

01:21:44.400 --> 01:21:45.740
Desert Island video game.

01:21:46.900 --> 01:21:50.640
It's like, it's the kind of game that's hard, but you can get better at it pretty quickly,

01:21:50.760 --> 01:21:55.200
I think. Got some solid mechanics, got some really cool levels, fun bosses.

01:21:56.060 --> 01:21:58.200
I'm going to go ahead and put it on the Essential List.

01:21:58.400 --> 01:22:01.160
All right. And so Sam has put it on.

01:22:01.320 --> 01:22:03.480
That is now one in favor.

01:22:03.680 --> 01:22:08.600
I want to remind everybody that you, according to the new rules of the 1988 season,

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:10.960
you need the majority.

01:22:11.340 --> 01:22:14.500
So three people must vote it into the Essential Games list.

01:22:15.020 --> 01:22:23.400
Sean? This game has so much more than what I expected out of the beginning of 88.

01:22:23.400 --> 01:22:29.120
I thought we were going to slog through a bunch more sort of half-baked shmups.

01:22:29.120 --> 01:22:35.520
I thought we were going to see a lot more of the third parties sort of dump shovelware on us.

01:22:35.520 --> 01:22:41.700
Um, I, I'm seeing growth in the utility of the system in terms of graphical fidelity,

01:22:41.700 --> 01:22:47.960
in terms of, uh, variety of gameplay, uh, situations.

01:22:47.960 --> 01:22:54.740
Uh, I mean, the, the one knock I have on the game, aside from some really frustrating points,

01:22:54.740 --> 01:23:04.700
is that it's a little short and it really relies on that, uh, difficulty to extend the gameplay,

01:23:04.700 --> 01:23:06.000
uh, experience.

01:23:06.000 --> 01:23:13.160
But aside from that, and I know that I have, I've harped on other games for that much, much worse,

01:23:13.360 --> 01:23:16.120
I will also put this game on the Essential Games list.

01:23:16.700 --> 01:23:17.840
Okay, that's two.

01:23:18.020 --> 01:23:21.980
It's just going to take one more to get this thing on the Essential Games list.

01:23:21.980 --> 01:23:27.880
Uh, keeping in mind that the Essential Games list I think now is down to 11 games.

01:23:27.880 --> 01:23:28.920
It was at 13.

01:23:29.420 --> 01:23:31.680
So, Joe, what's the stance?

01:23:32.160 --> 01:23:39.620
Uh, at this point it's also sort of, like, transcended the, the group of games that I consider good NES games.

01:23:39.780 --> 01:23:44.560
And this one, I just had a blast because it's just a good game to me.

01:23:44.560 --> 01:23:48.880
This is another one of those games that I would just play to play at any time,

01:23:48.920 --> 01:23:52.220
and I think it's because it is actually just good game design.

01:23:52.380 --> 01:23:53.520
It's aged really well.

01:23:53.700 --> 01:23:58.440
I mean, yes, there have been some flaws, but overall, it was a great experience.

01:23:58.660 --> 01:24:00.960
I will also put it on the Essential Games list.

01:24:01.100 --> 01:24:03.360
So that makes it an Essential game.

01:24:03.500 --> 01:24:06.800
Uh, I hope all of you are proud of yourselves for getting it there.

01:24:06.920 --> 01:24:08.400
I'm very proud. Okay, good.

01:24:08.620 --> 01:24:09.640
Yeah. Sam, still proud?

01:24:09.740 --> 01:24:12.720
Okay, good. That was Sam, for those of you wondering, not Kermit the Frog.

01:24:13.240 --> 01:24:14.540
Uh, I don't know.

01:24:14.560 --> 01:24:19.340
And, uh, you know, just so that we can keep it on the record, I will also vote.

01:24:19.520 --> 01:24:20.580
Uh, I won't give a whole spiel.

01:24:20.800 --> 01:24:27.200
I will just say that, uh, this game really surprised me, and for it to be a, like, first of its genre,

01:24:27.520 --> 01:24:30.300
even though, like Sean said, genres are kind of loose.

01:24:30.600 --> 01:24:37.900
Yeah. Uh, for this to be the first of its kind, to succeed so well, and I get that, like, there are a lot of these things on the arcade,

